"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThere is an important statement.\" [3092] \"What?\" I asked. \"Who said it? When?\" [3093] \"At the appropriate time, dear.\" [3094] \"Please tell me, Monsieur Poirot,\" the inspector politely returned to the topic. [3095] \"If you draw a circle around number 19, all the people inside it could have killed Mr. Corry. Mrs. Hemm, the Brandt couple, the MacNodon couple, Miss Watrous. But most importantly, some people have already been set up in the scene. Miss Pemerton may have killed him before going out at about 1:35 p.m., and Miss Webb may have been arranged to meet him, killing him before running out of the room to call the police.\" [3096] \"Ah,\" said the detective, \"now you're getting to the specifics.\" [3097] \"Of course,\" said Poirot, sliding his wheelchair, \"you, my dear Colwyn. You were also on the scene. Finding the lower half of the house on the upper half of the street.\" [3098] \"Oh, really?\" I said indignantly, \"What do you mean?\" [3099] \"I mean that I know all there is to know!\" Poirot declared pompously. [3100] \"And I'm the one who's still trying to tell you the whole thing!\" [3101] \"Murderers are usually arrogant,\" Poirot pointed out, \"and may well play games with you so you have a reason to laugh at me.\" [3102] \"If you go on like this, you'll convince me,\" I said. [3103] I began to feel a little uneasy. [3104] Poirot turned to Inspector Hercule. [3105] \"I say to myself, essentially, it's a simple case of murder. The appearance of the clock, the time advanced by an hour, the deliberate discovery of the body, all of this must be put aside. The essential thing is that an ordinary middle-aged man has died and that someone wanted him to die. If we knew who the dead man was, it would suggest who the murderer is. If he was a notorious con artist, we would have to find those he may have cheated; if he was a detective, we would look for those who had committed a crime under his nose; if he was a rich man, we would look among his heirs. But if we don't know who the man was, finding a suspect from this vast pool is a very daunting task.\" [3106] \"Without considering Miss Pemukh and Miss Hila Webber, who should not have been there, the answer is disappointing. I think only Mr. Lymes has something strange?\" Poirot looked at me inquiringly, and I nodded. \"Everyone has a reason to be trusted. Brand is a famous local architect, MacNorton is a professor at Cambridge University, Mrs. Hamm is the widow of a local auctioneer, and the Waterhouse siblings are respected locals. So let's go back to Mr. Curry. Where does he come from? What brought him to 19 Wilbraham Crescent? The neighbor, Mrs. Hamm, said something very revealing when she found out that the dead man was not living at number 19. 'Oh! I see. He came here to die. How strange.' She has a talent for seeing the essence of things, a talent that only those who are only concerned with their own actions and do not care about others possess. She summed up the whole case. Mr. Curry came to 19 Wilbraham Crescent to die. That's all there is to it!\" [3107] \"Her words suddenly startled me,\" I said. [3108] Poirot did not notice me. [3109] \" 'Dear, dear, dear, come to die.' Mr. Curry came and was killed. But that's not all. His identity cannot be confirmed, which is important. He had no wallet, no identification, and the brand name on his clothes had been torn off. But that's not enough. The card of an insurance agent named Curry was just a temporary measure. If the man's identity cannot be confirmed, he will eventually be given a false identity. It could be his brother, his sister, or his wife. As for the wife, Mrs. Rivale, just the name may have already aroused suspicion. There is a village called Curry-Rivale in Somerset, where I and a friend live nearby \u2013 I don't know what the two names suggest, Mr. Curry, Mrs. Rivale.\" [3110] \"At this point, the plan is almost clear, but what confuses me is why our killer assumes that the police will never be able to determine the actual identity of the deceased. If the man had no family, at least there would be a butler, a servant, or a business partner. This leads me to another assumption: no one knows that the man has disappeared. Another assumption is that he is not British, but only a traveler in this country. This fits with the fact that he had traces of having his teeth treated, but there was no record of any treatment.\" [3111] \"My mind began to form a vague picture of the victim and the killer. The case was carefully planned and skillfully executed, but now there is a flaw that the killer did not anticipate.\" [3112] \"What?\" Hercule asked. [3113] To my surprise, at this point Poirot tilted his head back dramatically and recited a poem:\n[3114] A nail lost, a shoe lost,\n[3115] A shoe lost, a horse lost,\n[3116] One war horse was lost, one war was lost,\n[3117] One war was lost, one empire was lost,\n[3118] All because one horseshoe nail was missing. [3119] He leaned forward. [3120] \"There may be many suspects for the murder of Mr. Curry. But there is only one who could have killed, or had a reason to kill, the girl Iona.\" [3121] We all turned to him at the same time. [3122] \"Let's consider Cavenish Typewriting Agency. There are eight girls working there. On September 9th, four of them were out on an errand, so they wouldn't be back until after lunch. Normally, they would have lunch between 12:30 and 1:30 p.m. The other four, Hilary Webb, Ingrid Brent, and two others, would have lunch between 1:30 and 2:30 p.m. But on that day, Ingrid Brent had a small accident on her way back to the office. Her shoe heel got stuck in the grating, making it difficult for her to walk. So she bought some bread and went straight back to the office.\" [3123] Poirot wagged his strong finger at us. [3124] \"We know that Ingrid Brent was worried about something. She tried to meet Hilary Webb outside the office, but failed. Could this be related to Hilary Webb? We couldn't find any evidence. She might have just wanted to ask Hilary Webb about something that was bothering her. But there was one thing that was clear. She wanted to talk to Hilary Webb outside the office.\" [3125] \"Her statement to the police, that morning, was the only clue we had to what was bothering her. She said, 'I don't know if what she said is true.' Three women testified that day. Ingrid might have been referring to Miss Pemshaw. Or, as we had previously surmised, she might have been referring to Hilary Webb. But there was a third possibility. She might have been referring to Miss Martin.\" [3126] \"Miss Martin? But her testimony only lasted a few minutes.\" [3127] \"Indeed, she only stated the phone call she received claiming it was from Miss Pemerton.\" [3128] \"Are you saying that Ina knew that the phone call was not from Miss Pemerton?\" [3129] \"My inference is even simpler. I believe there was no such phone call at all.\" [3130] He continued speaking:\n[3131] \"Ina's heel broke off. The grille was close to the office. So she returned to the typing pool quickly. But Miss Martindeer was in her own office, unaware that Ina had returned. As far as she was concerned, there was only herself in the office at that time. All she had to do was say that a call came in at 1:49. Ina did not know the severity of the situation at first. Hill went to see Miss Martindeer, who was told there was an appointment to go out. The nature and time of the appointment, Ina did not know. [3132] \"Then the news of the murder broke, and the story unfolded little by little. Miss Pemarho called and asked Hill to come to her. But Miss Pemarho said she had not made the call. The call was said to have come at 2:50 p.m. But Ina knew it was not true. There was no call at that time. Miss Martindeer must have been mistaken, but Miss Martindeer was sure she was not mistaken. The more Ina thought about it, the more puzzled she became. She had to ask Hill. Hill might know. [3133] \"Then the interrogation began. All the girls attended. Miss Martindeer repeated her statement about the call, but Ina knew that the clear evidence and precise time she provided were false. So she went to ask the officer to speak to the chief inspector. I think it's likely that Miss Martindeer overheard her question as she left with the crowd. Or perhaps she heard the girls talking about Ina breaking her heel at that moment, realizing the truth. Anyway, she followed the girl to Wilbraham Crescent. I wonder why Ina went there? [3134] \"Just to see the scene of the murder, I guess,\" Hakaste sighed. [3135] \"Yes, that's right. Maybe Miss Martindeer was talking to her, and they were walking down the street together when Ina casually expressed her doubts. Miss Martindeer decided to take immediate action. They happened to be walking past a phone booth. She said, 'This is very important. You must call the police immediately. Tell them we are going to see them right now.' Following someone's instructions was a habit Ina had formed. She went inside, picked up the phone, and Miss Martindeer followed her, standing behind her, tightening her scarf around her neck, and choking her to death.\" [3136] \"Did nobody see?\" [3137] Poirot shrugged. [3138] \"It should have been someone who saw! But it was exactly 1:00. Lunchtime. And people on New Moon Street were busy looking at this and that. This gave the bold and shameless person the opportunity to act.\" [3139] Hercule Poirot shook his head, incredulous. [3140] \"Miss Martin? I don't understand how she got involved in this case?\" [3141] \"Indeed, it was not immediately apparent. But there is no doubt that Miss Marple killed Ingrid. Oh, yes. Only she could have killed Ingrid, so she must be involved. From Miss Marple, I began to suspect that this murder was a Macbethian[1] one, a cruel and unimaginative boring woman.\u201d\n[3142] \"Lacking imagination?\" Hercule Poirot asked. [3143] \"Oh, yes, very lacking. But very efficient. A first-rate conspirator.\u201d\n[3144] \"But the motive? What was her reason for doing it?\" [3145] Hercule Poirot looks at me. He wags a finger. [3146] \"So, your neighbors' gossip was of no use to you, was it? I found a statement that intrigued me. Do you remember when talking about living abroad, Mrs. Brand said she liked living in Crofton because she had an aunt there. But Mrs. Brand actually had no aunt. She inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada, who was her only surviving relative in the family.\" [3147] Hercule Poirot sat up alertly. [3148] \"So you think \u2013\"\n[3149] Poirot leaned back in his chair, folding his fingers together. He half-closed his eyes, as if dreaming. [3150] \"For example, you are a man, a very ordinary, cautious man, with financial difficulties. One day, you receive a letter from a law firm saying that your wife has inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada. The letter was addressed to Mrs. Brand, but the problem is that the Mrs. Brand who received the letter was not the one mentioned in the letter. She was the second wife, not the first. Imagine how sad and frustrating that would be! But suddenly, there's a solution. Who would know that this was not Mrs. Brand? In Crofton, no one knew that Mr. Brand had been married before. His first marriage was many years ago, during the war, when he was overseas. His wife died soon after they married, and he remarried quickly. He had the original marriage certificate, various other documents, and photos of all the relatives in Canada. Everything was in order. Anyway, it was worth taking a risk. They tried, and succeeded. They went through all the legal procedures. The Brand couple became rich, and their financial difficulties ended \u2013\"\n[3151] \"However, a year later, something unexpected happened to them. What happened? I assume that someone from Canada came to this country and knew all the details about the first Mrs. Brand, and the truth was about to come out. He could be an elderly family executor or a close friend of the family, but whoever he is, he would know. Perhaps they thought they could avoid meeting. Mrs. Brand could pretend to be sick, or she could go abroad, but any similar thing would arouse suspicion. The visitor would insist on seeing the woman he had come to visit specially.\" [3152] \"So, they decided to murder him?\" [3153] \"Yes. Here, I think Mrs. Brand's sister is the mastermind behind the plot.\" [3154] \"You mean Miss Martin and Mrs. Brand are sisters?\" [3155] \"Only then does the story make sense.\" [3156] \"When I saw Mrs. Brand, she reminded me of someone,\" said Hercule Poirot, \"although their personalities were very different, but upon careful reflection, they were somewhat alike. But how could they hope to escape? Someone is missing. They would face interrogation \u2013\"\n[3157] \"If this person went abroad, perhaps just for a vacation, not for business, his schedule would not be fixed. Receiving letters from one place, then postcards from another. By the time people start to suspect, a long time may have passed. Who would associate the already-identified Harry Castleton with the wealthy tourist from Canada who never even set foot in the country? If I were the killer, I would go to France or Belgium for a carefree day of sightseeing, and then deliberately throw the dead man's passport in a train or a car, so that the interrogation could take place there.\" [3158] I unconsciously moved, and Poirot stared at me intently. [3159] \"Right?\" he said. [3160] \"Brand told me he had gone to Bournemouth for a day trip with a blonde woman, and I thought \u2013\"\n[3161] \"That's perfectly normal. Don't doubt it, it's his old habit.\" [3162] \"But these are just conjectures,\" Hercule countered. [3163] \"But you can do your own investigation,\" Hercule said. [3164] He picked up a hotel stationery sheet from the shelf in front of him and handed it to Hercule. [3165] \"You can write to Mr. Endebie at 10 Innisfree Gardens, SW7, who has promised to investigate for me in Canada. He is a well-known international lawyer.\" [3166] \"What about those clocks then?\" [3167] \"Oh! Those clocks! Those remarkable clocks!\" Poirot laughed. \"I think you will find that Miss Rosemary will be held responsible for them. Hence this case, as I said, is quite simple, only made to look fantastic. That clock that Miss Hyde took to be repaired. Did she leave it at the typewriter repair shop? Miss Marple took advantage of this to base her crazy talk on it, perhaps because of that clock, she chose Miss Hyde to be the one who discovered the body\u2014?\" [3168] Hercule suddenly exclaimed loudly:\n[3169] \"You still say this woman is boring and lacks imagination? When did she start planning all this?\" [3170] \"But these were not her plans. That's what makes it interesting. Everything was here, waiting for her. From the very beginning, I discovered this modus operandi, which I was familiar with because I had been reading about it. Very lucky. As Colin will tell you, this week I attended an auction of author's manuscripts. There were some works by Gary Greggson, and I didn't hold out much hope. But luck was on my side. Here\u2014\" Suddenly, he pulled out two old notebooks from the drawer of the table. \"\u2014it's all here! All the plots he planned to write in these books. He died before he could finish them. But Miss Marple, as his secretary, knew all the plots. She could use them to serve her purposes if she wanted to.\" [3171] \"But these clocks must have meant something \u2013 in Mr. Greggson's story, I think.\" [3172] \"Yes, that's right. His clocks were set at five minutes and four seconds, five minutes and seven seconds, and five minutes and five seconds. Together, they form a safe's password, 514547. The safe was hidden behind a replica of the Mona Lisa painting. Inside the safe, \"Poirot continued grumpily, \"was the Russian royal crown. And that's how everything was explained! It was Miss Marple's plot. An innocent girl being framed. Oh, yes, it was easy for Miss Marple. She just had to choose some local characters and make them act according to the plot. So these clear clues led you nowhere! Yes, she is a clever woman. I wonder if Mr. Greggson left her any inheritance? How did he die and what caused his death? I'm curious.\" [3173] Hercule didn't want to hear any more about the past. He put away the notebook and took the hotel business card from my hand. Staring at it, I realized how foolish I had been. [3174] \"Thank you, Poirot, sir,\" Hercule said. \"Your words have given us a lot of inspiration.\" [3175] \"The End\"\n[3176] \"If I have been of any help, I would be very happy.\" [3177] Polo appeared humble. [3178] \"I need to check all the facts -\"\n[3179] \"Of course, of course -\"\n[3180] After bidding farewell, Hercast left. [3181] Poirot turned to me. He frowned. [3182] \"Cheer up. I want to ask you, what's wrong? You look so gloomy.\" [3183] \"I realized how stupid I've been.\" [3184] \"Ah, that's alright. Many people are like that.\" [3185] \"But not you, Hercule Poirot!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[316] \"I'm sorry, I must have been quite ridiculous then.\" [317] \"Everyone does that,\" CaiLin said. [318] When XieLa got into the car, Hakasht also got in. After the car started moving, he turned to the young policeman. [319] \"I need those clocks in the living room to be packed carefully, except for the wall clock and the grandfather clock.\" [320] He made some other arrangements and then turned to his friend. [321] \"I'm going somewhere else. Want to come along?\" [322] \"Sounds good to me,\" said Colin. [323] Chapter 4\n[324] Colin Lumb's Narrative\n[325] \"Where are we going?\" I asked Dick Hakast. [326] He was talking to the driver. [327] \"Cavendish Manuscript Society. On the Strand, along the Coastal Arts Center, on the right.\" [328] \"Yes, sir.\" [329] The car drove off. There were now many people gathered around, curiously watching everything. The orange cat still sat on the post of \"Diana's Cottage.\" It was no longer licking its face, but sat upright, wagging its tail gently, and gazed at the heads of the people, with a look of disdain for humans. This was a privilege that only cats and camels had. [330] \"First to the manuscript society. Then to the cleaning woman, in that order,\" said Harkness. \"It's getting late. He glanced at his watch. \"It's after four o'clock.\" He paused, then continued, \"The girl is very pretty, isn't she?\" [331] \"Yes,\" I said. [332] He looked amused. [333] \"But she told a very strange story. The faster we find out the truth, the better.\" [334] \"Do you think she \u2014\"\n[335] He interrupted my sentence. [336] \"I always have an interest in those who discover bodies.\" [337] \"But that girl was so scared, she was on the verge of madness! If you had heard her screams\u2026\"\n[338] He gave me another mocking look and then said she was a charming girl. [339] \"How did you end up wandering around Wilbraham Crescent, Colin? Enamored by the classical Victorian architecture? Or do you have another purpose?\" [340] \"I have my reasons. I'm looking for number 61, but I can't find it. Maybe there's no such address?\" [341] \"It's here. The house numbers go up to 88.\" [342] \"But be careful, Dick, when I reach number 28, Wilbraham Crescent ends.\" [343] \"This always confuses strangers. If you turn right into Albany Road, then turn right again, you'll find yourself on the other half of Wilbraham Crescent. You see, the street is built back-to-back. One garden faces another garden.\" [344] \"Ah, I see,\" I said after he explained the peculiar layout in detail, \"just like in London's squares and gardens. Is it like Euston Square, or Cadogan? You start walking along one side of the square, and then suddenly it becomes a house or a garden. Even taxis get lost. Anyway, number 61 can be found. Do you know who lives there?\" [345] \"Number 61? Let me think. Oh, it might be an architect named Brand.\" [346] \"Oh, dear,\" I said. \"That's not good.\" [347] \"You're not looking for an architect?\" [348] \"Yes. I never thought it would be an architect. Unless - maybe he just moved in, just moved to this side?\" [349] \"Brand was born here, I think. He's a local, and he's been in business for a few years.\" [350] \"That's really disappointing.\" [351] \"He's not a good architect,\" Huckleberry said, \"because he uses inferior building materials, and the houses he builds look good on the outside, but when you move in, you find that everything is falling apart, out of place, and full of problems. It's commercial fraud, but he managed to escape punishment.\" [352] \"That's not good, Dick. I'm looking for an honest man.\" [353] \"A year ago, Brand got a lot of money. It was actually his wife who got it. She's Canadian, came here during the war, met Brand. Her family didn't want her to marry him, and they cut off contact with her after they got married. Last year, her grandfather died. His only son died in an air disaster, and due to war casualties and other events, Mrs. Brand became the sole survivor of the family. So he left the money to her. Therefore, Brand got out of the near-bankruptcy situation.\" [354] \"You seem to know a lot about Mr. Brand.\" [355] \"Oh, well, you know, when a person suddenly becomes rich overnight, the domestic tax department is always interested. They suspect that he has been falsifying and secretly saving money. So they started investigating, but nothing came out of it.\" [356] \"Anyway,\" I said, \"I'm not interested in people who get rich overnight. I don't want 'easy money'.\" [357] \"Is that so? Have you ever had any?\" [358] I nodded. [359] \"Is it over, or is it still going on?\" [360] \"It's a long story,\" I stammered, \"are we still having dinner together tonight, or is it canceled?\" [361] \"Oh, that's fine. The first thing we need to do is take action. We need to find out everything about Mr. Corey. Once we know who he is and what he's done, we can find out who killed him.\" [362] The Cavendish Agency is located on a busy street in the commercial center, which goes by the grand name of Wangfujing Street. [363] Like most other buildings, the houses on this street have been renovated in a Victorian style. On the right side of the street, there is a house of the same style, with the inscription \"Art Photographer Edwin Glen, specializing in children's portraits and wedding photos.\" To emphasize this slogan, the window displays a variety of enlargements of children of different ages, from infants to six-year-olds. These are probably to attract the attention of loving mothers; there are also several wedding photos, with a shy-looking young man next to a smiling girl. On the other side of the Cavendish Agency, there are some old-fashioned shops run by coal merchants. Beyond these shops, there are many old-style houses that have been demolished. There is a three-story building called \"Oriental Caf\u00e9\" that shines brightly. [364] Hakast and I climbed up four steps, walked through the open front door, and went along the right-hand door with the words \"Please enter\" engraved on it. It was a spacious room, and three young ladies were typing diligently. Two of them were typing, seemingly unaware of the strangers at the door. The third one was facing the door, with a telephone on her desk. She stopped, looking at us with a questioning gaze. She seemed to be sucking on some candy. After putting the candy in her mouth, she asked in a nasal tone,\n[365] \"What can I do for you?\" [366] \"Is Miss Martin there?\" Hakast said. [367] \"I think she's on the phone right now -\" Just then, \"click\" went the sound of the phone receiver being picked up, and the girl said, \"There are two gentlemen looking for you, Miss Martin.\" She looked at us and asked, \"What's your name?\" [368] \"Hakast,\" Dick said. [369] \"A Mr. Hakast is here to see you, Miss Martin.\" She hung up the phone, stood up, and said, \"This way, please.\" She led us to a door with a brass plaque that read \"Miss Martin.\" She opened the door, pushed it open with her back, and said, \"This is Mr. Hakast.\" She closed the door behind us. [370] Miss Martin looked up from her desk as we entered. She was a woman of about fifty, with neatly coiffed reddish-brown hair. She had alert eyes. [371] She surveyed us one by one. [372] \"Mr. Hakast?\" [373] Dick handed her his card. [374] Miss Martindale raised her light brown eyebrows in surprise, and it was clear she was somewhat displeased. [375] \"Detective Hakast, what brings you here, Detective?\" [376] \"I came to ask you some questions, Miss Martindale. I thought you might be able to help me.\" [377] From the tone of his voice, I judged that Dick was trying to charm her into talking. I was doubtful that Miss Martindale would fall for his charms. She was the kind of woman the French called \"difficult.\" [378] I decided to study the room's overall layout. On the wall behind Miss Martindale's desk hung some of her collection of autographed photos. I recognized one as a signature of Ariadne Oliver, a mystery novelist I knew a little about. \"Sincerely yours, Ariadne Oliver,\" was written in bold, black letters. \"Gratefully yours, Gary Gregson,\" was another signature, from a thriller writer who had died sixteen years ago. \"Forever yours, Miriam,\" was written on a photo of Miriam Hoge, a romance novelist. A photo of a bald, bashful-looking man looked quite sexy, with a small signature that read, \"Gratefully yours, Amadeus Levine.\" These mementos had one thing in common: most of the men held a pipe and wore tweed jackets, while the women looked serious, their faces almost buried in fur collars. [379] As I was observing, Inspector Hakast began to ask questions. [380] \"You hired a girl named Sheila Webb, didn't you?\" [381] \"Yes. She's not here at the moment\u2014\"\n[382] She pressed the buzzer and spoke to the receptionist outside. [383] \"Iris, has Sheila Webb returned yet?\" [384] \"No, Miss Martin-Dell, she hasn't yet.\" [385] Miss Martin-Dell switched off the buzzer. [386] \"She's out on business this afternoon,\" she explained. \"I thought she'd be back by now. She might have gone to the Heron Hotel at the end of the seafront, she has an appointment there at five o'clock this afternoon.\" [387] \"I see,\" said Hercule Poirot. \"Can you tell me something about Miss Webb?\" [388] \"I don't know much about her,\" said Miss Martin. \"She's been here, let me see, about a year now. Her work has been satisfactory.\" [389] \"Do you know where she worked before she came here?\" [390] \"If you're particularly interested, I can find out some more about her, Inspector Poirot. Her references are in the safe here. The only thing I can remember is that she worked in London, and she had very good references from her employers. I think it was a firm, but I can't remember which one. It was probably a property firm.\" [391] \"You say her work has been satisfactory?\" [392] \"Quite satisfactory,\" said Miss Martin, who was not the type to praise people without reason. [393] \"Not first-rate?\" [394] \"No, I should say not. She was a good typist, with an average speed; she had a fair education. She was a careful and accurate typist.\" [395] \"Do you know her personally, besides her work?\" [396] \"Not really. She lives with, I believe, her aunt.\u201d At this, Miss Martindale seemed a bit unsettled. [397] \"Detective Inspector Hatchet, may I ask why you're asking so many questions? Is this girl in trouble?\" [398] \"Not quite, Miss Martindale. Do you know Miss Millicent Pembroke?\" [399] \"Pembroke,\u201d said Miss Martindale, furrowing her yellow eyebrows. \"Yes, of course. She went to see Miss Pembroke this afternoon. The appointment was at three o'clock.\" [400] \"How was the appointment scheduled, Miss Martindale?\" [401] \"By phone. Miss Millechamp called and said she needed a stenographer's service and asked me to send Miss Webber over.\" [402] \"Did she specifically ask for Miss Sheila Webber?\" [403] \"Yes.\" [404] \"When was the call made?\" [405] Miss Martindale thought for a moment. [406] \"I took the call. It must have been around lunchtime. I think it was about a quarter to two. Anyway, it was definitely before two. Oh, I just remembered I have it written down in my note pad. The exact time was a quarter to three.\" [407] \"Did Miss Pemaroo call you herself?\" [408] Miss Martindale seemed a bit surprised. [409] \"I guess so.\" [410] \"But you're not familiar with her voice? You haven't met her?\" [411] \"Yes, I don't know her. She introduced herself as Miss Pemaroo and gave me her address, a number on Wilbraham Crescent. Then, as I said, she asked if Miss Webber, if she was free, would call on her at three o'clock.\" [412] This was said clearly and distinctly. I think Miss Daldry would be a very powerful witness. [413] \"Can you tell me exactly what happened?\" Miss Daldry asked impatiently. [414] \"Well, Miss Daldry, Miss Pemaroo denied making the call herself.\" [415] Miss Daldry stared in amazement. [416] \"Really! That's extraordinary.\" [417] \"In other words, you say someone made the call, but you can't be sure that it was Miss Pemshaw herself who made the call.\" [418] \"No, I certainly can't be certain. I don't know her, but frankly, I don't see the point of it? Is it some kind of prank?\" [419] \"Far more serious than that,\" Hercast said. \"Miss Pemshaw, or whoever she is, gave any reason for wanting to see Miss Webber specifically?\" [420] Miss Pemshaw thought for a moment. [421] \"She said Miss Webber had served her before.\" [422] \"Is that true?\" [423] \"Miss Webber said she didn't have the impression of having done anything for Miss Pemakh. But that can't be confirmed, Inspector. After all, these girls often go to different places to meet different people, so if it was months ago, they wouldn't remember. Miss Webber is not sure whether she had been there before. She only said she didn't remember going there. But in fact, Inspector, even if it's a prank, I don't understand why you're interested in this?\" [424] \"I was just about to say that. Miss Webber arrived at 19 Wilbraham Crescent when she went into the house and entered the living room. She told me that she had been told to do so beforehand. Is that right?\" [425] \"Yes,\" said Miss Martindale, \"Miss Pemakh said she would be late to come home, so she asked Miss Webber to go in and wait first.\" [426] \"When Miss Weaver entered the living room,\" continued Huckleberry, \"she found a dead man lying on the floor.\" [427] Miss Martin was shocked and speechless for a while. [428] \"You mean 'a dead man', detective?\" [429] \"A murdered man,\" said Huckleberry. \"To be precise, a stabbed man.\" [430] \"Oh, dear,\" said Miss Martin. \"This child must be devastated.\" [431] It seemed that Miss Martindale was the type of person who spoke conservatively and modestly. [432] \"Is there any connection between you and this Mr. R. H. Corey, Miss Martindale?\" [433] \"I don't think so, no.\" [434] \"Working at a small-town insurance company?\" [435] Miss Martindale still shook her head. [436] \"You should realize that I'm in a dilemma,\" the detective said. \"You said Miss Pemmeru called you and asked Miss Sheila Webber to come to her house at three o'clock, but Miss Pemmeru denied it. Miss Sheila Webber went there and then found a dead body. \" He waited expectantly. [437] Miss Martindale looked at him blankly. [438] \"It's impossible for all this to happen to me,\" she expressed disbelief. [439] Dick Hackett sighed and stood up. [440] \"This is quite good,\" he said politely. \"You've been running the company for a while, haven't you?\" [441] \"Yes, fifteen years. The company has grown well, starting small and expanding only when we had enough staff. I now employ eight girls who are always busy.\" [442] \"You must have written a lot of literature,\" Hakaste said, looking up at the photos on the wall. [443] \"Yes, initially my clients were mainly writers. I was Miss Pembroke's secretary for several years, and I worked for the well-known horror novelist Gary Greggson, Esq. In fact, it was his legacy that led me to start the Documentary Agency. I know many of his writer friends who referred me. I'm quite knowledgeable about authors' requirements, and I provide useful information services in necessary research areas - dates, citations, legal knowledge, details of police investigations, and detailed processes of poisoning, among others. Then there are foreign names, addresses, and restaurants, all prepared for authors who set their novels' plots in foreign locations. In the past, the public didn't pay much attention to the accuracy of novels, but now, readers will certainly write to the authors if they find any discrepancies in the work.\" [444] Miss Martindale stopped. Hakaste said politely, \"I'm sure you've achieved a lot in this field.\" [445] He walked towards the door, and I opened it for him. [446] In the outer office, the three girls were preparing to leave. The typewriter covers were closed. Irene, the receptionist, stood there pitifully, holding one high heel in one hand and the broken one in the other. [447] \"I just bought it a month ago,\" she sighed, \"and it was expensive. It's all because of that damn grate - at the corner, not far from the cake shop. That's where I lost the heel. I couldn't walk, so I took off my shoes and walked barefoot, holding a bunch of cakes in my arms. I don't know how I'm going to get home now, how I'm going to take the bus.\" [448] Just then, Irene saw us and looked uneasily at Miss Martindale, hastily hiding the depressing shoe. I think Miss Martindale is the kind of woman who doesn't appreciate high heels, as she herself wears sensible flat shoes. [449] \"Thank you, Miss Martindale,\" Hakaste said. \"I apologize for taking up so much of your time. If you suddenly think of anything -\"\n[450] \"Of course,\" Miss Martindale interrupted him firmly. [451] After we got into the car, I said:\n[452] \"Despite your skepticism, what Helia Webber said is true.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nPerhaps Inna had a reason to know, or suspected that Miss Waite had impersonated Miss Pembroke's voice, calling to arrange for a stenographer to go to number 19? [1855] More conjecture. [1856] Or, of course, Sheila Webb herself...\n[1857] Detective Hardcastle's hand reached for the phone. He dialed the hotel where Colin Lumb was staying. He soon heard Colin's voice. [1858] \"This is Hardcastle. At what time did you and Sheila Webb have lunch today?\" [1859] There was a silence on the other end of the phone, then,\n[1860] \"How did you know we had lunch together?\" [1861] \"Just a guess. You were together, weren't you?\" [1862] \"Why shouldn't I have lunch with her?\" [1863] \"No reason. I just wanted to know the time. Did you go straight to lunch after the interrogation?\" [1864] \"No. She went shopping first. We met at the Chinese restaurant on Market Street at one o'clock.\" [1865] \"I see.\" [1866] Hakaste glanced down at his notepad. Iona Brent died between 12:30 and 1:00. [1867] \"You don't want to know what we had for lunch?\" [1868] \"Stay calm. I just want to know the exact time. For the record.\" [1869] \"I see. I hope so.\" [1870] Another silence ensued. Hakaste tried to lighten the mood\u2014\n[1871] \"If you don't have anything planned for tonight\u2014\"\n[1872] The other side interrupted 