e saw me in the interrogation room?\" [1902] \"Hmm...,\" the detective smiled slightly, quickly covering up the smile as he realized Miss Pema didn't like his attempts to get close to her. \"No one will ever know the reason now. Perhaps she just wanted a signature, or something similar.\" [1903] \"A signature!\" Miss Pemukh's voice was filled with contempt, and then she said, \"Yes... yes, I suppose you're right. Such things do happen.\u201d Then she shook her head briskly. \"But I can assure you, Inspector Hakaste, that such a thing has not happened today, and since I've been back from the interrogation, no one has come here.\u201d\n[1904] \"Well, thank you, Miss Pema. We thought it best to check every possibility.\" [1905] \"How old is she?\" Miss Pemaux asked. [1906] \"I believe she's nineteen years old.\" [1907] \"Nineteen? So young. Very young... poor child. Who could want to harm such a young girl?\" [1908] \"But it did happen,\" Detective Hardcastle said. [1909] \"Was she pretty... charming... seductive?\" [1910] \"No,\" Detective Hardcastle said. \"I think she wished to be, but she wasn't.\" [1911] \"Then it wasn't for that reason,\" Miss Pembroke said, shaking her head again. \"I'm sorry, I'm sorry, Detective Hakast, I couldn't help you.\" [1912] He left, as before, once again impressed by Miss Pembroke's personality. [1913] 2\n[1914] Miss Wutherspoon was also at home. She was still the same, suddenly opening the door as if deliberately tempting someone to do something they shouldn't. [1915] \"Oh, it's you!\" she said. \"In fact, I've told your people everything I know.\" [1916] \"I'm sure you've answered all the questions asked of you,\" Detective Hakast said. \"But not all questions can be answered at once, you know. We wanted to know some details.\" [1917] \"I don't understand why,\" Miss Walters said, looking at him with a nearly critical eye, as if it were all his fault. \"Please come in, please come in. You can't stand on the doormat all day. Come in,