re was a strange buzzing noise, not a busy signal, so I went to the phone booth.\" [1932] Poirot stood up. [1933] \"I'm sorry to disturb you like this, Miss Whitehouse, but this girl coming to New Moon Street might really have been visiting someone and gone to a house not far from here.\" [1934] \"So you should ask along New Moon Street,\" said Miss Whitehouse. \"I think she's most likely to have gone to the one next door \u2013 I mean Miss Pemarue.\" [1935] \"Why do you think that's the most likely?\" [1936] \"You said she was a stenographer from Cavendish Typewriting Agency. Of course, if I'm not mistaken, I heard just a few days ago, on the day that man was killed, Miss Pembury had a stenographer come to her.\" [1937] \"She denied it.\" [1938] \"Well, if you ask me,\" Miss Wattershouse said, \"I would say she might be a little odd - I mean Miss Pembury. I think, perhaps, she might have called the typing agency and asked for a stenographer to come to her. Then, perhaps, she forgot all about it.\" [1939] \"Do you think she could be the murderer?\" [1940] \"I never thought she'd go around killing someone or anything like that. I know someone was killed in her house, but I never suggested that had anything to do with Miss Pembury. No. I was just thinking she might have some odd preferences, like some other people. I know a woman who keeps calling the candy store and ordering a dozen sugar cookies. But she really doesn't need them, and when they're delivered, she says she didn't order them. Just that kind of thing.\" [1941] \"Of course, anything's possible.\" Hercast said. He left Miss Warton after bidding her goodbye. [1942] He thought she was just trying to clear herself of suspicion. In other words, if she believed someone saw the girl go into her house, then suggesting that the girl went into No. 19 becomes a clever way of covering up in this particular case. [1943] Hercast glanced at his watch and decided he had time to visit the Cavenish Document Printing Office