ime, Miss Martindeer.\" he said. [1954] \"Time? I guess you think you have all the time in the world because a silly girl died. The next one to be murdered will be another of these girls.\" [1955] \"I think you don't need to worry about that, Miss Martindeer.\" [1956] \"I suppose you didn't think this girl would be killed this morning, did you, Detective? If you had, you would have taken preventive measures to protect her. You would have been just as surprised when one of my girls was killed or found in a terrible compromising situation. The whole thing is so unusual, incredible! You must admit it's a mad scheme. That means, maybe what you read in the papers is true. Like those clocks. I noticed that nobody mentioned them at the trial this morning.\" [1957] \"We had to keep the questioning to a minimum this morning, Miss Martindale. It was just a postponement of the trial, you know.\" [1958] \"All I want to say is, Miss Martindale, that you have to take action on this.\" [1959] \"Didn't Ina give you any hints? Didn't she show any concern about something? Didn't she talk to you about it?\" [1960] \"I don't think she would have talked to me even if she was worried,\" said Miss Martindale. \"But what was she worried about?\" [1961] This was exactly what Inspector Hercule Poirot wanted to know. He knew he wouldn't get an answer from Miss Martin. So he said:\n[1962] \"I want to talk to each girl here. I understand Ina Brent wouldn't confide her fears or worries to you, but she might have confided in her colleagues.\" [1963] \"That's probably true,\" Miss Martin said. \"They're always gossiping - these girls. As soon as they hear my footsteps in the corridor, all the typewriters start working. But what were they doing before that? Chattering, chattering, never stopping! \"After a moment's pause, she said, \"There are only three girls in the office now. Do you want to talk to them now? The others are out on business. I can give you their names and addresses if you need them.\" [1964] \"Thank you, Miss Martin.\" [1965] \"I hope you talk to them alone,\" Miss Martin said. \"If I stand here and watch, they'll feel uncomfortable. Because, you understand, then they'll have to admit they've been gossiping and wasting time.\" [1966] She rose from her chair and opened the door, entering the outer office. [1967] \"Girls,\" she said, \"Inspector Hakast wants to talk to you. You stop what you're doing. Talk to the inspector properly and see if you can help him find the killer of Ina Brent.\u201d\n[1968] She returned to her office and slammed the door. Three girls, looking frightened, stared at the inspector. One was blonde, wearing glasses, and looked trustworthy but not very intelligent. One looked mischievous, with dark skin and hair, her hair disheveled as if she had just been through a storm. Her eyes darted around the room, and he suspected that what she said might not be entirely truthful. Everything had to be handled carefully. [1969] He spoke calmly and casually. [1970] \"Have you all heard about what happened to your colleague Ina Brent?\u201d\n[1971] Three heads nodded vigorously. [1972] \"By the way, how did you find out?\" [1973] They looked at each other, deciding who should speak first. After unanimous agreement, the blonde girl spoke. Her name was apparently Janet. [1974] \"Ina didn't come back to work at two o'clock as usual,\" she explained. [1975] \"The cat was furious,\" said the black-haired girl, Melvin, before correcting herself, \"I mean, Miss Martin.\" [1976] The third girl chuckled. \"The cat is our nickname for her,\" she explained. [1977] \"Not a bad name,\" the detective thought. [1978] \"She can be quite terrifying when she's angry,\" Melanie said, \"She'll scold you fiercely. She asked us if Ina had said she wouldn't come to the office today and said she should at least give a reason.\" [1979] The blonde girl said, \"I told Miss Martinfield that she went with us to the courthouse, but we didn't see her after that, and we don't know where she went.\" [1980] \"That's true, isn't it?\" Hakaste asked, \"You don't know where she went after leaving the courthouse.\" [1981] \"I suggested we all go to lunch,\" Morin said. \"But she seemed preoccupied. She said she wasn't sure if she should eat lunch or buy something to eat in the office.\" [1982] \"So, she meant she'd come back to the office after that?\" [1983] \"Oh, yes, of course. We all have to come back to work.\" [1984] \"Has anyone noticed anything different about Ina Brent these past few days? Does she seem troubled, like she's thinking about something? Has she told you what's bothering her? If you know anything, please tell me.\" [1985] They looked at each other, their exchange lacking any attempt at concealment. It seemed to be merely vague speculation. [1986] \"She's often been in a state of mind, \" said Molly. \"She's been making mistakes and getting things mixed up. Her reactions are always a beat behind.\" [1987] \"It seems like there's always some kind of trouble with Iona,\" said the smiling girl. \"Remember when her high heel fell off a few days ago? Things like that always happen to Iona.\" [1988] \"I remember,\" said Hakast.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1] Chapter 1\n[2] Colin Lamb's Narrative\n[3] 1\n[4] According to police records: At 2:59 PM on September 9th, I was walking west on Wilbraham Crescent. This was my first time here, and I must admit, everything about the place left me bewildered. [5] For some time now, I've had a feeling, and this feeling has been growing stronger and stronger, almost impossible to shake off. That's just how I felt. [6] I was looking for a house numbered 61, and I thought, maybe I won't find it. I had carefully found houses from 1 to 35, and just then, I discovered that Wilbraham Crescent had reached its end. A road called Albany Avenue rudely blocked my path. I turned around. There were no houses to the north, only a wall. Behind this wall, there were towering modern apartments, and it was clear that the entrance was on another road. It was impossible to find a way from here. [7] As I walked, I paid attention to the house numbers I passed by \u2013 24, 23, 22, 21, Diana's Cottage (which must be number 20, with an orange cat washing itself on the gatepost), and 19. [8] The door to number 19 suddenly opened, and a young girl rushed out, then ran up the path. I thought a missile's speed was nothing compared to hers. She ran with such a sharp cry that she looked even more like a missile. The sound was high-pitched and pitiful. The girl ran through the gate and collided with me, almost knocking me off the sidewalk. She not only collided with me but also grabbed me tightly, desperately clinging to me. [9] \"Take it easy,\" I said, gently shaking her when I regained my balance, \"Now take it easy.\" [10] The girl's emotions finally calmed down. Although she was still holding on to me tightly, she was no longer screaming, but only panting heavily, gasping and sobbing. [11] In this situation, I must say that my reaction was not quick enough. I asked her what happened. Realizing that my tone was too weak, I immediately changed it. [12] \"What happened?\" [13] The girl took a deep breath. [14] \"Over there!\" She pointed to the place behind her. [15] \"Then what?\" [16] \"There was a man lying on the floor... dead... she was about to step on him.\" [17] \"Who? How did this happen?\" [18] \"I think, because she's blind. And there was blood on him.\" [19] \"Indeed, there was blood.\" I said, looking at the stain on my sleeve. \"Now I'm the same.\" I pointed to the stain. Thinking about the current situation, I sighed. \"You should take me there to see.\" [20] But she began to shake her head vigorously. [21] \"I can't go, I can't go... I don't want to go back there.\" [22] \"Maybe you're right.\" I looked around. There didn't seem to be a place to put a girl who was about to faint. I gently guided her, letting her lean against the iron railing. [23] \"Stay here,\" I said, \"don't leave until I come back. I won't be long.\" I leaned forward and if you feel uncomfortable, put your head between your knees, it will feel better.\" [24] \"I, I think I'm okay now.\" [25] Although she said so, she was still a little shaken, but I didn't want to talk to her too much. I gently patted her shoulder, comforted her, and then walked quickly up the path. I went through the door into the house, hesitated slightly in the hallway, observed the left door, which was an empty restaurant, and then walked through the foyer into the opposite living room. [26] The first thing I saw was an old woman with gray hair sitting on a chair. When I went in, her head turned quickly towards me and said,\n[27] \"Who came?\" [28] I immediately realized that the woman's eyes were blind. Her eyes looked straight at me, then swept past me and landed on the back of my left ear. [29] I said directly:\n[30] \"A young girl ran up the street, saying that someone died here.\" [31] I felt that what I said was absurd. In this clean room, the woman sat with her hands folded on the chair, looking so calm. It seemed impossible to find a dead man here. [32] But she immediately answered. [33] \"Behind the sofa,\" she said. [34] I went around the corner of the sofa. Then I saw the open arms, the vacant eyes, and the congealed blood clots. [35] \"How did this happen?\" I suddenly asked. [36] \"I don't know.\" [37] \"So, who is he?\" [38] \"I don't know.\" [39] \"We have to call the police.\" I look around. \"Where's the phone?\" \"I don't have a phone.\" [40] I stare at her intently. [41] \"Are you living here? Is this your house?\" [42] \"Yes.\" [43] \"Can you tell me what happened?\" [44] \"Of course. I came back with my shopping -\" I noticed the shopping bag thrown on the chair near the door. \"I came home and immediately sensed that there was someone else in the house. A blind person is very sensitive to such things. I asked who was in the house. No one answered me. I only heard someone breathing heavily. I went towards the source of the sound. Then someone yelled out - someone had died, I was about to step on the body. Then someone screamed past me, running out of the room.\" [45] I nodded. They both told the same story. [46] \"What did you do then?\" [47] \"I walked carefully until my foot touched something.\" [48] \"What happened next?\" [49] \"I squatted down. I felt a man's hand. It was cold and there was no pulse\u2026 I got up and walked over here, sat down and waited for someone to come. It won't be long, someone will come. That young woman, whoever she is, will call for help. I think it's best not to leave this house.\" [50] I was shocked by the woman's calmness. She didn't scream or panic in the house, she just sat there quietly waiting. She was rational, but we still had to take action. [51] She questioned:\n[52] \"Who are you?\" [53] \"My name is Colin Lamb. I just happened to pass by.\" [54] \"Where did the young girl go?\" [55] \"I let her rest near the entrance. She was scared. Where is the nearest place to call for help?\" [56] \"Follow this path for about fifty yards, and there's a phone booth just before the corner.\" [57] \"Yes, I remember passing by there. I'm going to call the police. You\u2014\" I hesitated. [58] I didn't know whether to say \"Are you going to stay here?\" or \"Are you okay?\" [59] She spared me the decision. [60] \"You should bring the girl inside,\" she said firmly. [61] \"I don't know if she'll come,\" I said skeptically. [62] \"Of course, don't let her come into this room. Tell her to wait in the dining room next to the entrance. Tell her I'm brewing tea for her.\" [63] She approached me. [64] \"But can you do that?\" she asked. [65] A slight smirk appeared on her face. [66] \"My dear young man, since I moved into this house fourteen years ago, I have been cooking in the kitchen myself. Being blind doesn't mean I'm useless.\" [67] \"I'm sorry, I was too ignorant. Perhaps I can ask for your name?\" [68] \"Millee Pema-Chhoe \u2013 Miss.\" [69] I left the room and walked out along the path. The girl saw me and struggled to get up. [70] \"I, I think I'm almost better now.\" [71] I helped her stand up, smiling,\n[72] \"That's good.\" [73] \"There, there, someone died, right?\" [74] I immediately nodded. [75] \"Indeed. I'm going to the phone booth to report the incident to the police. If I were you, I would wait in the house. Go to the restaurant and wait on your left as you enter. Miss Millechamp is brewing tea for you.\" I raised my voice to prevent her from immediately contradicting me. \"Wait in the restaurant, on your left as you enter. Miss Millechamp is brewing tea for you.\" [76] \"So that's Miss Pemakh, is it? Can she see?\" [77] \"Yes, she is. This has certainly shocked her, but she has been very rational about it. Come on, I'll take you in. Having a cup of tea while waiting for the police will make you feel better.\" [78] I put my arm around her shoulder and led her down the path. After settling her comfortably at the table, I rushed to the phone booth. [79] 2\n[80] A cold voice said, \"Croedin Police Station.\" [81] \"Is Inspector Hatchet there?\" [82] The voice suddenly became cautious:\n[83] \"I don't know. Who is this?\" [84] \"Tell him it's Colin Loom.\" [85] \"Please wait.\" [86] I waited. Then Dick Hakast's voice came over the line. [87] \"Is that Colin? It's been a while. Where are you?\" [88] \"Crofton. To be precise, I'm on the floor of 19 Wilbraham Crescent. There's a man lying there, dead, I think stabbed. Probably died about half an hour ago.\" [89] \"Who found him? You?\" [90] \"No, a stranger. A girl came running out of the house, almost knocking me over. She said there was a man lying on the floor of the house, dead. A blind woman almost stepped on his body.\" [91] \"You're not pulling my leg, are you?\" Dick asked skeptically. [92] \"I admit, it sounds incredible, but that's the truth. The woman is the owner of the house, Miss Mirella Pembleton.\" [93] \"The one who almost tripped over the dead man?\" [94] \"Not like you think. If she was blind, she wouldn't have noticed the dead man lying there.\" [95] \"I'll be right there. Wait for me there. How did you take care of the girl?\" [96] \"Miss Pemah is brewing tea for her.\" [97] Dic commented that the arrangement sounded somewhat soothing. [98] Chapter Two\n[99] At 19 Wilbraham Crescent, the police officers had arrived. A forensic expert, a photographer, and a fingerprint examiner were present. They worked in an orderly manner, with each person performing their designated task efficiently. [100] The last to arrive was Inspector Hakast, a tall man with expressive eyebrows and a stern expression. He observed that all the work he had arranged was proceeding smoothly. He glanced at the corpse, exchanged a few brief words with the forensic expert, and then went to the dining room. There, he found three people seated with three empty teacups in front of them. They were Miss Pemah, Colin Lum, and a tall girl with brown curls. The girl's eyes were wide with fear. \"Very nice,\" the inspector remarked in his usual witty manner. [101] He introduced himself to Miss Pema. [102] \"Detective Inspector Dick Hakast.\" [103] He knew something about Miss Pema, despite their lines of work never intersecting. He had seen her a few times before, knowing her to be a teacher at the Allenburg Institute, teaching blind script to disabled children. The discovery of a man dead of murder in such a clean, simple house seemed improbable. But often the most improbable things are the most common. [104] \"This is a truly awful thing, Miss Pema,\" he said, \"and I'm afraid it's quite a shock to you. I need to get a clear understanding of the whole incident from you. I know -\" he glanced quickly