et. That's what's strange about it.\" [125] \"It is quite unusual. Well, let's not discuss that for now. When did you get here?\" [126] \"It must have been around three o'clock, because that cuckoo clock\u2014\" She immediately stopped. Her eyes widened. \"How strange. It's so strange. I didn't really pay attention to the time then.\" [127] \"Did you notice anything, Miss Webber?\" [128] \"How could I have not noticed those clocks?\" [129] \"What about those clocks?\" [130] \"The cuckoo clock struck three, but the others were almost an hour ahead. How strange!\" [131] \"That is strange,\" the detective replied. \"So, when was the first time you found the body?\" [132] \"It wasn't until I turned around the sofa that I noticed it. There he was, lying there. It was terrible, so terrible\u2026\"\n[133] \"Terrible! I agree. Do you know the man? Have you seen him before?\" [134] \"No, I haven't.\" [135] \"Are you sure you've never seen him? He might look different now than when you last saw him. Think carefully. Are you absolutely certain you've never seen him before?\" [136] \"Absolutely.\" [137] \"Alright. That's enough about that. So, what did you do next?\" [138] \"What did I do?\" [139] \"Yes.\" [140] \"Why are you asking? I didn't do anything, absolutely nothing. I couldn't have done anything.\" [141] \"I understand. You didn't touch him at all?\" [142] \"Oh, yes, I did. I touched him to see if... I mean, just to confirm, but he was so cold, and then, then my hand got covered in blood. It was so horrible, sticky.\" [143] She started shaking. [144] \"Okay, okay,\" Detective Hakast said in a soothing tone, \"Let's move on from that. Forget about the blood. What happened next?\" [145] \"I don't know... Yes, she came back then.\" [146] \"You mean Miss Pemakho?\" [147] \"Yes. I didn't realize it was Miss Pemakho. She just happened to come in with a shopping bag.\" [148] \"So, what did you say?\" [149] \"I think I didn't say anything... I tried to speak, but I couldn't make a sound. It felt like my throat was being squeezed.\" [150] The detective nodded. [151] \"Then, she said, 'Who's there?' and she came around the sofa, and I thought, I thought she was going to step on him. That's when I screamed... Once I started screaming, I couldn't stop. I don't know how I just ran out of the house and out the front door.\" [152] \"Like a ghost,\" the detective remembered Colin's description. [153] Shira Webber looked at him with eyes full of pain and fear, and unexpectedly said,\n[154] \"I'm sorry.\" [155] \"There's nothing to be sorry about. You did well. Don't think about it anymore. Oh, and one more thing, why were you in that room?\" [156] \"Why?\" She looked confused. [157] \"Yes. You may have arrived a few minutes early, I think, and then you rang the doorbell. But if no one answered, how did you get in?\" [158] \"Oh, that. She told me to come in.\" [159] \"Who told you?\" [160] \"Miss Pemaroo.\" [161] \"But I thought you hadn't spoken to her.\" [162] \"Yes, we hadn't. Miss Martindale told me to wait in the sitting room on the right in the hall.\" [163] Hakast pondered, \"So that's how it is!\" [164] Sheila Weber timidly asked:\n[165] \"And is there anything else?\" [166] \"I think so. I'd like you to stay here for about ten minutes, in case I think of anything else to ask you. Then, I'll send a police car to take you home. What's your family situation?\" [167] \"My parents are dead. I live with my aunt.\" [168] \"What's her name?\" [169] \"Mrs. Lott.\" [170] The detective rose and held out his hand. [171] \"Thank you very much, Miss Webber,\" he said. \"Have a good night's sleep and rest well. You need plenty of sleep after all this.\" [172] She timidly smiled at him and then entered the dining room.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[592] I whistled. [593] \"That's really strange. What's going on?\" [594] \"These damn idiots! I mean, I'm one of them too, I think -\" (Dick is a very honest person) \"You must be very careful, otherwise things will get worse. Well, yesterday the clocks were still properly placed in the living room. I let Miss Pemahyu touch them all to see if she was familiar with them. She said she wasn't. Then they came to move the body.\" [595] \"Then what?\" [596] \"I went out to the front gate to check on other things, then came back inside and spoke to Miss Pemarthy in the kitchen, telling her I had to take these clocks and would give her a receipt.\" [597] \"I remember. I heard you say that.\" [598] \"I then told the lady I would arrange for her to be driven home, and I asked you to take her to the car.\" [599] \"Yes.\" [600] \"Even though Miss Pemarthy said she didn't need a receipt, as the clocks weren't hers, I still gave her one. Then I came to find you. I told Edward to carefully pack the clocks in the living room, and bring them here. All the clocks except the cuckoo clock were packed. Of course, there was also the grandfather clock. That's where I made my mistake. I should have been very specific and told them there were four clocks. Edward said he went in immediately and did what I said. But he insisted that there were only three clocks, besides the two fixed ones.\" [601] \"There wasn't much time between all of this,\" I said, \"which means\u2014\"\n[602] \"It could have been Pemahux. She might have wrapped the clock up and taken it directly to the kitchen after I left the house.\" [603] \"But what about the motive?\" [604] \"We need more information. Was there anyone else? Was it the girl who did it?\" [605] I thought for a moment. \"I don't think so. I\u2014\" I stopped suddenly, remembering something. [606] \"So she might have,\" Hakaste said, \"continue. When was this?\" [607] \"Just as we came out, walking towards the police car,\" I said glumly, \"she forgot her gloves. I said 'I'll go get the