"What was the autopsy result?\" [643] \"Well, it was a sharp instrument, like a kitchen knife.\" [644] \"Doesn't that favor Miss Pemerton? A blind woman would have a hard time killing a man. She really is blind, isn't she?\" [645] \"Yes, she is blind. We checked, and it's consistent with what she described. She was a math teacher at a rural school in the north, and she went blind sixteen years ago. She received training in Braille, and eventually found a job at the Allenberg Institute.\" [646] \"Maybe she has a mental illness?\" [647] \"Because of her obsession with clocks and insurance agents?\" [648] \"It's simply indescribable, too strange. My strong curiosity makes me say this, 'like Ariadne Oliver at her worst, or the late Gary Gregson at the height of his career...'\"\n[649] \"Go on and express your emotions. You're not the poor wretch in charge of the criminal investigation team. You don't have to flatter the senior police officers or the police commissioner, or anyone else.\" [650] \"Okay! Maybe we can find useful clues from the neighbors.\" [651] \"No telling,\" Hercule Poirot said coldly, \"If the man was killed in the front garden and then the two masked men carried him into the house. No one looked out the window or noticed anything. This isn't the countryside, our luck would be terrible. Wilbraham Crescent is a residential area for the upper class. The women who finish their work at one o'clock are already home. There's not even a handcart on the street \u2013\"\n[652] \"No elderly people sitting by the window all day, sickly?\" [653] \"We wish there were, but that's impossible.\" [654] \"What about number 18 or 20?\" [655] \"Number 18 is occupied by Mr. Waitehouse \u2013 an assistant to Messrs. Gastford and Swinburne, the lawyers, and his sister who comes to take care of him in his spare time. As for number 20, I only know that a woman lives there who owns eighteen cats. I don't like cats \u2013\"\n[656] I told him, being a policeman is no easy task. And then we set off. [657] Chapter 7\n[658] Mr. Walters paced up and down the steps of 18 Wilbraham Crescent, anxiously looking back at his sister. [659] \"Are you sure you're right?\" Mr. Walters said. [660] Miss Walters huffed in anger. [661] \"I really don't understand what you mean, James.\" [662] Mr. Waltershouse looked apologetic. He must have often expressed regret, as his face seemed to bear the traces of such an expression. [663] \"Well, I just thought, dear, of what happened next door yesterday\u2026\"\n[664] Mr. Waltershouse was preparing to go to his law firm. He was a gentlemanly man with gray hair, slightly hunched, and pale complexion, not pink, but not sickly looking either. [665] Miss Waltershouse was tall and slender, a woman who was serious and intolerant of anyone who was not serious. [666] \"James, just because someone was murdered next door, does that mean I have a reason to be murdered today?\" [667] \"Well, Edith,\" said Mr. Warthouse, \"it depends on the kind of person the killer is?\" [668] \"Do you really think, James, that someone was wandering up and down Wilbraham Crescent, looking for a house to murder someone in? It's sacrilege.\" [669] \"Sacrilege, Edith?\" Mr. Warthouse was astonished. He would never dare say such a thing. [670] \"The Passover memories,\" said Miss Warthouse, \"remind you that it comes from the Bible.\" [671] \"I think that's a bit of a stretch, Edith,\" said Mr. Walterhouse. [672] \"I should have been thinking about who might come here to try and murder me,\" said Miss Walterhouse, becoming quite agitated. [673] Her brother pondered silently, the idea of someone trying to murder her seemed quite impossible. If he were choosing a victim, he would never choose his sister. If someone did attempt it, the assailant would likely be beaten back by a poker or door latch, covered in blood, and handed over to the police in such a humiliating and unusual circumstance. [674] \"I just mean,\" he said, looking more apologetic, \"yes, there have been some unsavory characters around here.\" [675] \"We know very little about what's been happening,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"Rumors are spreading all over the place. This morning, Mrs. Black said some strange things.\" [676] \"I hope so,\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch. He had no intention of listening to the idle gossip from the maids. His sister never wasted time debunking such sensational stories; she only took a moment of amusement from them. [677] \"Some people say,\" Miss Watrous said, \"that this man was an accountant or a trustee at Allerton College, and he came to Miss Pembroke to inquire about some discrepancies in the accounts.\" [678] \"And Miss Pembroke killed him?\" Mr. Watrous seemed amused. \"A blind woman? Are you sure\u2014\"\n[679] \"She quietly wrapped a wire around his neck and strangled him,\" Miss Watrous said. \"He didn't suspect anything, you know. Who would suspect a blind woman? I don't believe it,\" she continued. \"I'm sure Miss Pembroke is a woman of excellent character. We may disagree on many things, but that doesn't mean I consider her a suspect. I just think her views are narrow and stubborn. There are other issues besides education. All these new grammar schools, they look so peculiar, with classrooms made of glass. You might think they were built for growing cucumbers or tomatoes. I'm convinced they're not suitable for children during the summer months. Mrs. Hazes told me herself that her daughter Susan doesn't like the new classrooms at the school. She said because of these glass windows, you always want to look outside, and you can't concentrate on your studies.\" [680] \"Oh, dear!\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch again. \"Well, I'm afraid I'll be late. Goodbye, dear. Take care of yourself. Remember to lock the door!\" [681] Miss Watrous sniffed disdainfully as her brother left. She closed the door and started up the stairs. She paused to think, walked over to her golf bag, and took out a n