t you?\" [693] \"A murder in my own backyard is never ignored,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"I've even turned away a few reporters who came asking questions.\" [694] \"You turned them away?\" [695] \"Of course.\" [696] \"You did the right thing,\" Hakaste said. \"Of course, they like to sneak back in, but I'm sure you can handle it with your abilities.\" [697] For such praise, Miss Houseworth allowed herself a hint of pleasure. [698] \"I hope you don't mind us asking you the same questions,\" Hakaste said. \"But if you saw anything that could be helpful to us, I'm sure we would be grateful for your information. At that time, I think you were in the house?\" [699] \"I don't know when the murder happened,\" Miss Houseworth said. [700] \"We think it was between 1:30 and 2:30 PM.\" [701] \"I was in the house, yes, indeed.\" [702] \"Where was your brother?\" [703] \"He doesn't come for lunch. Who was murdered? The local papers haven't mentioned anything.\" [704] \"A stranger?\" [705] \"It seems so now.\" [706] \"You're not telling me that for Miss Pemerton he's a stranger too?\" [707] \"Miss Pemerton told us very confidently that she had no appointment with this particular guest, and she didn't know who he was.\" [708] \"She couldn't be so sure,\" said Miss Watrous. \"She can't see.\" [709] \"We described the person's appearance to her in great detail.\" [710] \"What did he look like?\" [711] Mr. Huckleberry took a photograph from the envelope and handed it to her. [712] \"That's the man,\" he said. \"Do you recognize him?\" [713] Miss Warton looked at the photo. \"No, no... I'm sure I've never seen him before. Oh dear. He looks like a very respectable person.\" [714] \"He does look respectable,\" said the detective. \"He looks like a lawyer or a businessman.\" [715] \"Yes, that's true. There's nothing about this photo that suggests any misfortune. He looks like he's sleeping.\" [716] Hakast didn't tell her that this photo was chosen from among many others, the one that was least likely to cause discomfort. [717] \"Death can sometimes be a peaceful thing,\" he said. \"I think this particular man never expected what was about to happen next.\" [718] \"What did Miss Pembroke have to say about this?\" Miss Walters asked. [719] \"She was very surprised.\" [720] \"That's strange!\" Miss Walters commented. [721] \"Now, please help us out, Miss Walterhouse. If you can remember yesterday, try to think if you saw anything outside the window, or if you happened to be in your garden between twelve-thirty and three o'clock, tell us what happened during that time?\" [722] Miss Walterhouse recalled. [723] \"Yes, I was in the garden... let me think. It should have been before one o'clock. I went into the house from the garden at a quarter to one, washed my hands, and then sat down to lunch.\" [724] \"Did you see Miss Pemahku enter or leave the house?\" [725] \"I think she came back. I heard the creak of the gate. Yes, usually after twelve-thirty.\" [726] \"Did you speak to her?\" [727] \"Oh, no. It was just the sound of the gate that made me look up. That's when she usually comes home. I think she finished her class.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe looked up at me and asked for my opinion on the trial. [1735] I told him that I thought it was well-organized. [1736] \"We're good at such things in our country.\" [1737] \"What do you think of the medical evidence?\" [1738] \"It's quite a coincidence. Why didn't you tell me before?\" [1739] \"You left, did you consult your experts?\" [1740] \"Yes, I did.\" [1741] \"I don't remember his appearance too well. He seemed to have a mustache. Quite thick, I remember.\" \"Yes, indeed,\" I chimed in, \"he was proud of it.\" [1742] \"He must be very old.\" [1743] \"He is old, but not senile,\" I said. [1744] \"Why are you so determined to see him? Just because of human nature's innate kindness?\" [1745] \"You have a detective's penchant for suspicion, Dick! That's mainly the reason. But I also admit, curiosity got the better of me. I wanted to hear his opinion on our special action plan. You know, he always says that he can easily crack a case just by sitting in a chair, putting his fingertips together symmetrically, closing his eyes, and thinking. I wanted to get him to reveal his hand.\" [1746] \"Did he show you this from beginning to end?\" [1747] \"He did it.\" [1748] \"So, what did he say?\" Dick asked curiously. [1749] \"He said,\" I told him, \"this is definitely a very simple murder case.\" \"Simple, my goodness!\" Hakastar stood up, \"Why do you say it's simple?\" [1750] \"According to him,\" I said, \"because the whole plan was too complicated.\" [1751] Hakast shook his head. \"I don't understand what he means,\" he said. \"It sounds like a story of young people fighting in Chelsea [1], but I don't get it. Is there anything else?\" [1752] \"Well, he told me to talk to the neighbors. I assured him that we had already done so.\" [1753] \"Considering the results of medical evidence, the findings on the neighbors' side now seem more important.\" [1754] \"According to the assumption, he was first anesthetized by someone else and then moved to number 19 to be killed?\" [1755] These words sounded familiar, and I couldn't help but be startled. [1756] \"It's like, what's her name, the cat lady's words. She said something very interesting that surprised me.\" [1757] \"Those cats,\" Dick shivered, continuing. \"We've found the weapon, by the way. Just yesterday.\" [1758] \"You found it? Where?\" [1759] \"At the house of the cat-owner. It's probably where the killer threw it after the murder.\" [1760] \"No fingerprints, I suppose?\" [1761] \"It was wiped clean. It's a regular knife, not used much. Looks like it was sharpened recently.\" [1762] \"So, things are as you say. First, they anesthetized him, then he was taken to the 19th? By car?\" [1763] \"He was probably transported from a house with an adjacent garden to the 19th.\" [1764] \"That's a bit risky, isn't it? Impossible to do that?\" [1765] \"It takes courage,\" Hakaste said, \"and a good understanding of the habits of the neighbors around. It's more likely to be transported by car.\" [1766] \"That's also risky. People would notice a car.\" [1767] \"No one saw it. But I agree that the killer couldn't guarantee they wouldn't be seen. Someone must have noticed a car parked in front of number 19 that day \u2013\"\n[1768] \"I want to know if anyone noticed,\" I said. \"Everyone sees a car and thinks it's normal. Unless, of course, it's a very luxurious car, something quite unusual, but that's not possible \u2013\"\n[1769] \"It was lunchtime. You understand, Colin, this brings to mind Miss Milla Pemmerho? It's hard to imagine a strong man being killed by a blind woman. But if he was anesthetized \u2013\"\n[1770] \"In other words, 'if he came here to die.' As Mrs. Black said, he came here on time, drank a sherry or a cocktail. The anesthetic in the drink took effect quickly, and then Miss Pemmerho acted. She washed the glasses, neatly placed the body on the floor, threw the knife into the neighbor's garden, and went out as usual, as if nothing had happened.\" [1771] \"She called the Cavendish Typewriting Office on the road \u2014\"\n[1772] \"Why would she do that? Why specifically ask for Miss Hill Weaver?\" [1773] \"I wish we knew. \" Hakast looked at me. \"Does she know? Does the girl know herself?\" [1774] \"She said she didn't.\" [1775] \"She said she didn't,\" Hakast repeated flatly. \"I was wondering what you thought of it.\" [1776] I paused for a moment. What was I thinking? I had to make a decision. The truth would come out. If Sheila was really the way I believed her to be, she wouldn't be hurt. [1777] I took a postcard out of my pocket and slid it across the table to Hakast. [1778] \"Sheila received this from the post office.\" [1779] Hakast examined it closely. It was one of a set of postcards featuring London buildings, with the Central Criminal Court as the subject. Hakast flipped it over. On the right was the address printed neatly: Miss R.S. Webber, Sussex, Crofton Road, 14 Palmerston Road. On the left was also printed, with the words \"Remember\"! Underneath was written 4:13. [1780] \"Four thirteen,\" Hakast said. \"That's the time the alarm clock showed that day.\" He shook his head. \"A picture of the Central Criminal Court, two words 'Remember', a time, four thirteen. This must be connected to something.\" [1781] \"She said she didn't know what it meant,\" I added. \"I believe her.\" [1782] Hercule Poirot nodded. [1783] \"Continue investigating. We may find something out.\" [1784] \"I hope so.\" [1785] The atmosphere between us became a bit awkward, so I said, to break the tension, \"I'm going to make some tea. Would you like some?\" [1786] \"You must have a lot of files to go through?\" [1787] \"As usual, most of them are useless. The deceased had no criminal record, and his fingerprints were not on file. In fact, all these letters are from people who claim to have recognized him.\" [1788] \"Dear Sir, the man pictured in the paper is almost certainly the one I saw at Waterloo Station the other day, getting on a train. He was muttering to himself, and seemed very excited, and I thought it was odd at the time.\" [1789] \"Dear Sir, I think this man looks very much like my husband's cousin John. He went to South Africa and may have come back. He left with a small beard, but of course, he could have shaved it off.\" [1790] \"Dear Sir, I saw the man in the paper last night on the Underground, and I thought to myself, something must have happened.\" [1791] \"Of course, women always think they recognize their husbands. In fact, it seems they can't clearly remember what their husbands look like. There are also hopeful mothers who think they still recognize their 