s. [1810] Chapter 17\n[1811] An hour and a half later, Chief Inspector Hakast sat down behind his desk, taking a sip of tea and letting out a slight sigh. He still looked cold and angry. [1812] \"Excuse me, sir, Pierce said he has something to say to you.\" [1813] Hakast stood up. [1814] \"Pierce? Oh, alright. Let him in.\" [1815] A young police officer with a worried expression walked in. [1816] \"I'm sorry, sir, I think I should tell you.\" [1817] \"Hmm? Tell me what?\" [1818] \"After the interrogation, sir. I was on duty at the door. This girl, the one who was killed. She came over and talked to me.\" [1819] \"She talked to you, did she? What did she say?\" [1820] \"She said she wanted to talk to you, sir.\" [1821] Hakaste sat up straight, suddenly alert. [1822] \"She wanted to talk to me? Did she say why?\" [1823] \"No, sir. I'm sorry, sir, if I had just done as she asked. I asked her if she could leave a message, or if she could come back to the police station later. You were busy, talking to the county police chief and the coroner, and I thought\u2014\"\n[1824] \"Damn it!\" Hakaste whispered, \"Why didn't you tell her to wait for me?\" [1825] \"I'm sorry, sir.\" The young man blushed. \"If I had thought of it, I would have. But I thought it wasn't important. I think she thought it wasn't urgent either. She just said she had some doubts.\" [1826] \"Doubts?\" Hakast said. He was silent for a moment, going over the events in his mind. This was the girl who had passed him on the way to Mrs. Rotten's house; the girl who wanted to see Shira Weber; the girl who had recognized him and hesitated whether to stop and talk to him as she passed by. She must have had something on her mind. Yes, that was it. She had been thinking about something. He had missed it. He had been too preoccupied with trying to find out more about Shira Weber's background and had ignored an important clue. What was she doubting? Why? Now, perhaps they would never know the answer. [1827] \"Go on, 