e man wearing it was never the same person. The coat was switched, but the man who switched it never spoke to Rake, and Rake never spoke to him. We wanted to know more technical details. This plan was flawless, and the timing was precise. This person is indeed very smart.\" [555] \"That's why you're still hanging around the Portsmouth Naval Base, right?\" [556] \"Yes, we know the location in the naval base and the location in London. We know when and where Rackin collects his salary and how he collects it. But there's a gap between these two points. There's a subtle and delicate organization between them. That's where we want to know more, because that's where the brainpower comes into play. There's a great command center here, a route that will make you doubtful not once, but seven or eight times. It's a superb plan.\" [557] \"Why did Rackin do all this?\" Hakast asked curiously, \"For political ideals? Promoting individualism? Or for quick money?\" [558] \"He's not an idealist,\" I said. \"I think it's 'just for money'.\" [559] \"If you had started from that angle earlier, wouldn't you have found him faster? He must have spent the money. He doesn't save money.\" [560] \"Oh, that's right! He spent all the money. In fact, I was a bit hasty in dealing with him.\" [561] Hakast nodded understandingly. [562] \"I see. You fell once and then gradually used him. Right?\" [563] \"More or less. Before we found him, he had already taken out some valuable intelligence, so we let him leak more intelligence, obviously very valuable. In my workplace, we sometimes have to act like idiots.\" [564] \"I don't like your job, Colin,\" Hakast said thoughtfully. [565] \"It's not the kind of exciting job people think it is,\" I said. \"In fact, it's usually monotonous, but not just boring. Now people have a feeling that there is nothing that is truly secret. We know their secrets, and they know ours. Our agents are often their agents, and their agents are often ours. In the end, whoever is the double agent, it becomes a nightmare! Sometimes, I think, everyone knows each other's secrets, but they set up conspiracies and pretend not to know.\" [566] \"I see what you mean,\" Dick said kindly. [567] He looked at me curiously. [568] \"I know why you're still in Potterbury. But Croton is ten miles away from Potterbury.\" [569] \"Actually, my real purpose is,\" I said, \"New Moon Street.\" [570] \"New Moon Street?\" Hakast looked puzzled. [571] \"Yes. Or, the moon, the new moon, the rising moon, etc. I started my exploration from Portbury. There's a pub called 'New Moon' there. I spent a lot of time there. It sounds a bit crazy. Then there's 'Moon and Stars', 'Rising Moon', 'Happy Scythe', and 'Cross and Crescent' \u2013 that one's in a place called Simde. Nothing came of it. So I gave up on the moon pubs and started studying crescent-shaped streets. There are a few of them in Portbury. There's Lancelbury Crescent, Addington Crescent, Lifemide Crescent, and Victoria Crescent.\" [572] Suddenly, I saw a confused expression on Dick's face and started laughing. [573] \"Don't look so bewildered, Dick. There's something real waiting for me to start.\" [574] I took out my wallet and pulled out a piece of paper from it, handing it to him. It was a hotel stationery paper, on which a sketch was drawn. [575] \"This is something a boy named Hambley had in his wallet. He put a lot of effort into the case of Racking. He's a good guy, really good. He was hit by a car in London and died. The driver fled. No one saw the license plate. I don't know what this is, but it's something Hambley hastily wrote down or copied, thinking it was important. It made him think of something? Did he see or hear anything? Maybe something related to the moon or the new moon, and the number 61 and the capital letter M. After his death, this note fell into my hands. I don't know what I'm looking for, but I'm sure I'll find something. I don't know what 61 represents, or what M means. I've been busy for three weeks now, within a radius centered on Portbury. I haven't found anything. Cromwell is within my plans. That's it. To be honest, Dick, I don't have much hope for Cromwell. There's only one New Moon Street there, which is Wilbraham New Moon Street. Before asking you if you found any information that can help me, I'm going to walk along Wilbraham New Moon Street first and see what the number 61 looks like. That's what I did this afternoon, but I couldn't find number 61.\" [576] \"I told you, 61 is occupied by a local architect.\" [577] \"That's not what I'm looking for. Are there any foreigners living there?\" [578] \"There might be. Many people do these days. If there are, they'll be listed. I'll check for you tomorrow.\" [579] \"Thank you, Dick.\" [580] \"I'll be doing routine inquiries to the neighbors on either side of 19 tomorrow. Asking if they've seen anyone go to that house, and so on. I'll go to the house behind 19, the one whose garden connects with 19's. I seem to remember that 61 is right behind 19. If you'd like, I can take you along.\" [581] I eagerly agreed. [582] \"I'll be your RAMS officer, in charge of taking notes.\" [583] We agreed to meet at the police station at 9:30 the next morning. [584] 2\n[585] On the second day, I arrived at the appointed time and found my friend seething with anger. [586] After he sent away his sulking subordinate, I cautiously asked him what had happened. [587] For a moment, Hakaste seemed unable to speak. After a while, he blurted out, \"It's those damn clocks again!\" [588] \"Clocks again? What's the matter this time?\" [589] \"One of them is missing.\" [590] \"Missing? Which one?\" [591] \"The leather travel clock. The one with 'Rosemary' written on it.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIs this the man who came to ask me if I wanted to buy a set of fourteen-volume new encyclopedias? Or the one who came with the latest vacuum cleaner? We have no connection with him. He went to the front garden, where he annoyed my husband, who was planting bulbs. You know, he didn't want to be disturbed, but the man kept talking about his product. You know, how it comes up and down to clean curtains, how it cleans doorsteps, stairs, cushions, and does a thorough cleaning. He almost mentioned everything. Then Angus looked up at him and said, 'Can it plant bulbs?' I must say it made me laugh out loud, as it forced the man to suddenly step back and leave.\" [1120] \"So, you're absolutely sure this man is the one in the photo?\" [1121] \"Well, no, I can't be sure,\" said Mrs. MacNutt, \"because it was a younger man. Let me think. But anyway, I think I've seen this face before. Yes. The more I look at it, the more sure I am that he came here and made me buy something.\" [1122] \"Maybe insurance?\" [1123] \"No, no, not insurance. My husband took care of all that. We are fully insured. No. But anyway, yes, the more I look at this photo\u2026\"\n[1124] Hackett was not encouraged by this, on the contrary, perhaps even worse. From his rich experience, he thought Mrs. MacNutt was too eager to have seen someone related to the murder case. So the longer she looked at the photo, the more certain she would be that she could remember the man in the photo. [1125] He sighed. [1126] \"I think he was driving a big truck,\" said Mrs. McNutt, \"but I can't remember when I saw him. I think it was a bakery truck.\" [1127] \"Did you see him yesterday, Mrs. McNutt?\" [1128] Mrs. McNutt's face fell. She brushed her greasy gray curls back from her forehead. [1129] \"No, no, not yesterday,\" she said, \"at least -\" She stopped, \"I think not.\" Then her face brightened again. \"Maybe my husband will remember.\" [1130] \"Is he home?\" [1131] \"Oh, he's out in the garden,\" she said, pointing out the window. An older man was pushing a wheelbarrow along a path. [1132] \"Perhaps we should go out and talk to him.\" [1133] \"Of course, come this way.\" [1134] She led them through a side door and into the garden. Mr. MacNorton looked flushed and sweaty. [1135] \"These two gentlemen are from the police station, Angus,\" his wife panted. \"They want to ask about the murder at Miss Pemmican's house. Here's a picture of the deceased. Do you know, I'm sure I've seen him before. Was it last week when he came asking if we had any antiques to dispose of? Was it that man?\" [1136] \"Let me see,\" Mr. McNaughton said, \"just hold it up for me. I've got mud on my hands and can't touch anything.\" He addressed Mr. Harkness, \"You know about my gardening, don't you?\" [1137] \"Yes, Mr. Harkness said so,\" Mr. McNaughton grunted. [1138] \"Not Mrs. Lymes, I hope.\" [1139] \"No, it was Mr. Brand.\" [1140] Angus McNaughton snorted. [1141] \"Brand doesn't know what gardening is,\" he said, \"he just digs holes and then plants begonias, geraniums, and Cape tulips. That's not what I call gardening. Those flowers would survive in the park. Are you interested in shrubs, Inspector? Of course, it's not the season for planting shrubs, but I have a couple of shrubs here that you might be surprised to see I've kept alive. It's said that shrubs thrive best in Devonshire and Cornwall.\" [1142] \"I'm afraid I'm not much of a gardener,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1143] Mr. McNaughton looked at him as an artist might look at someone who doesn't understand art but knows what they like. [1144] \"Perhaps I should start talking about the unpleasant topic,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1145] \"Certainly. It happened yesterday, I was in the garden.\" [1146] \"Really?\" [1147] \"Well, I mean, when I heard that girl's scream, I was here.\" [1148] \"What were you doing?\" [1149] \"Well,\" Mr. MacNoughton said, startled, \"I didn't do anything. In fact, I thought it was those two noisy kids from the Lymeses' house. They're always yelling, screaming, making a racket.\" [1150] \"But the scream came from a different direction, didn't it?\" [1151] \"Yes, if those boys had stayed in their own garden. But they don't, you know. They're always jumping over other people's fences and hedges. They're chasing poor Mrs. Black's cats all over the place. Nobody's watching them, that's the trouble. Their mother's a nice, quiet sort. Of course, when there's no man in the house, the children get out of hand.\" [1152] \"I know Mr. Rimmers often goes abroad.\" [1153] \"He's an engineer, as far as I know,\" Mr. McNaughton said vaguely, \"always on the move. Dam building, or pipelines, or something like that. I'm not criticizing, dear, but I mean this kind of dam building, or laying oil pipelines, or whatever. I don't know the real story. He rushed off to Sweden a month ago, leaving a lot of work for the children's mother\u2014cooking and housework. Well, no wonder they're getting wild. They're not bad at heart, they just need discipline.\" [1154] \"Did you see anything besides hearing the scream? I mean, when did you hear it, by the way?\" [1155] \"I'm not sure. I usually take off my watch when I go into the garden. The other day, the water pipe was broken, and it took me a while to fix it. What time was that, dear? You heard it too, didn't you?\" [1156] \"Probably around half past two, at least half an hour after we had lunch.\" [1157] \"I see. When do you have lunch?\" [1158] \"At half past one,\" Mr. McNaughton said. \"If we're lucky. Our Danish girl doesn't have a good sense of time.\" [1159] \"And then what? Do you take a nap?\" [1160] \"Sometimes. But not today. I need to finish what I started. I cleared up a lot of waste and added it to the compost pile.\" [1161] \"Great, making compost,\" said Hakaste seriously. [1162] Mr. McNuton immediately brightened up. [1163] \"Absolutely right. Nothing beats homemade compost. I've convinced many people of that. Using chemical fertilizers is like self-destruction, isn't it? Let me show you.\" [1164] He enthusiastically pulled Hakaste's arm and pushed the wheelbarrow along the path to the fence separating his garden from No. 19. The compost was surrounded by lilacs, making it look enticing. Mr. McNuton pushed the wheelbarrow to a small shed next to it. The shed was neatly arranged with various tools. [1165] \"You've organized everything so well,\" commented Hakaste. [1166] \"You must take care of your tools,\" McNotton said. [1167] Hakaste closely observed the 19th garden. On the other side of the fence was a path lined with roses, leading to the corner of the house. [1168] \"Did you see anyone in the 19th garden or peering out the window while you were making compost?\" [1169] McNotton shook his head. [1170] \"I didn't see anyone. Sorry I couldn't help you, Detective.\" [1171] \"You know, Angus,\" his wife said, \"I'm sure I saw a shadowy figure hiding in the garden of number 19.\" [1172] \"I don't think you did, dear,\" her husband said firmly. \"I didn't either.\" [1173] \"That woman kept saying she saw this and that, as if she could spot anything,\" Hakaste grumbled as they got back in the car. [1174] \"Do you think she recognized the person in the photo?\" [1175] Hakaste shook his head. \"I doubt it. She's just imagining she's seen him. I know this kind of witness. When I asked her to clarify, she couldn't answer, right?\" [1176] \"That's right.\" [1177] \"Of course, she could have said she saw him on the bus or somewhere else, sitting across from her. But if you ask me, I think it's just her wishful thinking. What do you think?\" [1178] \"I agree.\" [1179] \"We didn't learn anything,\" Hakast sighed. \"Of course, some things seem strange. For example, Mrs. Black, no matter how she tried to associate herself with the cat, shouldn't be completely unaware of her neighbor Miss Pema, which seems impossible. And she was so confused about the murder case, showing no concern.\" [1180] \"She's just a muddled woman.\" [1181] \"No brains!\" Hakaste said. \"When you encounter such a silly woman, fires, thefts, and murders happening around them, they won't even pay attention.\" [1182] \"Her wire mesh fence is quite effective. The Victorian shrubs almost hide everything from view.\" [1183] They returned to the police station. Hakaste grinned at his friend:\n[1184] \"Well, Detective Blue, I can now let you go home.\" [1185] \"No more interviews?\" [1186] \"Not for now. I'll be visiting a few more places later, but not with you.\" [1187] \"All right, then. Thank you for this morning. Will you have my notes typed up? Here's the folder.\" \"You said the interrogation is tomorrow, isn't it? What time?\" [1188] \"Eleven o'clock.\" [1189] \"Good. I'll be back then.\" [1190] \"You're leaving?\" [1191] \"I'm going to London to give a report tomorrow. I can guess who it is.\" [1192] \"Don't say anything.\" [1193] Hakaste grinned. [1194] \"Say hello to that guy for me.\" [1195] \"Also, I might go see an expert,\" said Colin. [1196] \"An expert? What kind of expert? What's going on?\" [1197] \"Nothing, just stupid. Not that kind of expert. He's in your line of work. He's my father's friend, and he's my friend. You know, he loves these kind of things. It excites him. I think he needs a little stimulation.\" [1198] \"Scotland Yard?\" [1199] \"No. A private detective, a friend of my father's, and also a friend of mine. Your bizarre work would be right up his alley. He enjoys these things. It would excite him. I think he needs some stimulation.\" [1200] \"What's his name?\" [1201] \"Hercule Poirot.\" [1202] \"I've heard of him. I thought he was dead.\" [1203] \"He's still alive. But I feel he has nothing to do. It's worse than being dead.\" [1204] \"You're a strange guy, Colin. Having such strange friends. \" \"And you're no different,\" Colin said with a grin. [1205] ***\n[1206] [1] In English, the words \"dam\" (as in a dam) and \"damn\" are pronounced the same. [1207] Chapter 12\n[1208] After sending Colin away, Inspector Hercule looked at the neatly written address on his notepad and nodded. Then he quietly put the notebook back in his pocket and began to deal with the pile of daily files on his desk. [1209] He worked all day. He sent someone out to buy him coffee and sandwiches, received a report from Sergeant Grey - no useful leads were found. No one recognized Mr. Kuri's photo at the train station or bus station. The check of the clothing in the laboratory was also fruitless. The suit was made by a skilled tailor, but the label of the tailor's shop had been cut off. Was the information that Mr. Kuri wanted to hide his identity or the identity of the killer? The detailed report on the deceased's teeth had been distributed to various places, which was likely the most useful clue. It would take some time, but eventually, there would be a result. Unless, Mr. Kuri was a foreigner? Hercule imagined, it was likely that the deceased was French, but his clothes were not French-made, and there were no laundry shop labels to inquire about. [1210] Inspector Hercule was not impatient. Confirming the identity is often a slow process. But in the end, someone would come forward to confirm it, perhaps the laundry shop owner, the dentist, the doctor, or the landlady. Mr. Kuri's photo would be sent to various police stations and would be published in newspapers. Soon, Mr. Kuri's true identity would be discovered. [1211] Meanwhile, there was other work to be done, not just Mr. Kirby's case. Detective Hakast worked non-stop until 5:30 PM. He glanced at his watch again and thought it was a suitable time to visit a few more places. [1212] According to Inspector Gray's report, Ms. Sheila Webber had returned to Cavenish Typewriting Agency and had an appointment with Professor Puddy at the Heron Hotel at 5:00 PM, and she would not leave until after 6:00 PM. [1213] What was the name of the aunt? Mrs. Rotten, Mrs. Rotten. Living at 14 Palmerston Road. He did not drive his police car but chose to walk the shortcut. [1214] It was said that Palmerston Road used to be a lifeless road. Detective Hakast noticed that most of the houses had been converted into apartments or small houses. As he turned the corner, a girl was walking along the sidewalk towards him. She seemed to hesitate for a moment. Detective Hakast was thinking about other things at the time, and he thought she was going to ask him for directions. But if that was the case, why did she change her mind and continue to walk past him? He wondered why he suddenly thought of shoes. Shoes... No, just one shoe. This girl made him feel vaguely familiar. Who was she? Someone he had seen recently... maybe she recognized him and wanted to talk to him? [1215] He stopped and looked back at her. She was walking quickly by now. The problem was, her face had no clear and distinct features, making it hard to remember, unless there was a special reason. She had blue eyes, fair skin, and a slightly open mouth. The mouth. This made him think of something else. What was she doing with her mouth? Speaking? Putting on lipstick? No. He was a bit annoyed with himself. Detective Hakast prided himself on being able to remember people's facial features. He could say that he would never forget the people he had seen in the defendant's and witness's stands, but after all, he didn't just deal with those people. He couldn't remember, for example, every waitress who had served him. He couldn't remember every bus conductor. So he decided not to think about it for now. [1216] He arrives at number 14. The door is half open, and there are four bells beside it with names written underneath. He sees Mrs. Rudd. She lives in the ground-floor apartment. He goes in and rings the doorbell on the left of the hall. After a few minutes, he hears movement. Soon, footsteps are heard from inside. A tall, thin woman opens the door, her black hair disheveled, wearing a large apron, and seems to be out of breath. There is an onion smell coming from the house, obviously from the kitchen. [1217] \"Is this Mrs. Rudd?\" [1218] \"What do you want?\" She looks at him suspiciously and seems a little annoyed. [1219] He thinks she is about forty-five years old and looks a bit like a gypsy. [1220] \"What do you want?\" [1221] \"Could I trouble you for a moment to discuss something?\" [1222] \"Well, what is it? I'm very busy now.\" She spoke quickly, \"Are you a reporter?\" [1223] \"Of course not,\" Detective Hackett said sympathetically, \"I imagine you've been bothered by reporters quite a bit, haven't you?\" [1224] \"Yes, they keep knocking on the door and ringing the bell, asking all sorts of stupid questions.\" [1225] \"That can be quite irritating, I know,\" Detective Hackett said, \"I hope we can help alleviate some of that for you, Mrs. Rutter. I'm Detective Hackett, by the way, in charge of the case that has reporters bothering you. We'll do our best to prevent that from happening, but unfortunately, we can't control the press, you know, they have their own rights.\" [1226] \"It's inconsiderate of them to bother others like that,\" Mrs. Rudd said, \"claiming they have to report the news for the public. I've seen their reports in the newspaper, and they're all full of nonsense. As far as I know, they just make up facts. Please come in, Detective.\" [1227] She stepped back a few steps, and Detective Hakast walked through the doorway. She closed the door. There were several envelopes on the doormat. Mrs. Rudd was about to bend down to pick them up when Detective Hakast politely beat her to it. He glanced at the envelopes, checking the addresses, before handing them over to the hostess. [1228] \"Thank you.\" [1229] She placed them on the table in the hall. [1230] \"Go ahead to the living room, please? I'll be right there. The food in the kitchen is almost ready.\" [1231] She hurried off to the kitchen. Detective Hackett glanced one last time at the letters on the hall table. One was addressed to Mrs. Rotten, and the other two were for Miss R.S. Webber. He followed Mrs. Rotten's directions into the room. It was a small, messy room with old, stained furniture and unidentifiable stains and spots everywhere. There was an eye-catching, expensive Venetian cast glass dish, an abstract model, two colorful velvet cushions, and an exotic-looking large ceramic shallow dish. He thought that either the aunt or the niece had some taste. [1232] Mrs. Rotten returned, panting even more than before.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[173] \"Take care of Miss Webber, Colin,\" the detective said. \"Now, Miss Permshaw. Could you come here, please?\" [174] Hakast stretched out his hand to guide Miss Permshaw, but she firmly came over herself, feeling her way with her fingertips, and found a chair close to the wall, pulling it out a foot's distance and sitting down. [175] Hakast closed the door. Just as he was about to speak, Miss Millicent Permshaw suddenly asked, \"Who is the young man?\" [176] \"His name is Colin Lamb.\" [177] \"He has already told me that. But who is he? How did he get here?\" [178] Hakaste looked somewhat surprised at her. [179] \"When Miss Weaver screamed and ran out of the house saying there was a murder, he happened to be passing by the street. After confirming what happened inside the house, he called us and we let him wait back.\" [180] \"You call him Colin.\" [181] \"You are very observant, Miss Pemahox (the word 'meticulous' is not quite appropriate, but seems to be the most suitable one). Colin Lamm is my friend, but we haven't seen each other for a long time. He is an ocean biologist.\" [182] \"Oh! So that's it.\" [183] \"Now, Miss Pemahox, if you could tell me about this strange incident, I would be very happy.\" [184] \"I would be glad to. But there is nothing much to say.\" [185] \"I suppose you have been living here for some time, haven't you?\" [186] \"I have been living here since 1950. I am... my profession is a teacher. When I was told that my deteriorating vision was incurable and that I would soon become blind, I studied Braille and other skills that could help the blind, hoping to become an expert in this field. I now have a job at the Allenberg Institute for Disabled Children.\" [187] \"Thank you. Now, tell me about what happened this afternoon. Are you waiting for a guest?\" [188] \"No.\" [189] \"I will describe the physical features of the deceased to see if you can recall any specific person. His height is between 5 feet 9 inches and 10 inches, around sixty years old, with gray hair and brown eyes, clean-shaven beard, a thin face, and a strong chin. He is in good physical condition but not overweight. He was wearing a dark gray suit and well-groomed hands. He could be a banker, accountant, lawyer, or a professional in some industry. These descriptions remind you of anyone you know?\" [190] Miss Pemarcho carefully thought about the description before answering. [191] \"I cannot pinpoint it exactly because these are very common descriptions, and many people have these features. Perhaps I have seen or met someone like that in some occasion, but I cannot confirm who it is.\" [192] \"Have you received any letters recently from anyone planning to visit you?\" [193] \"Absolutely not.\" [194] \"Good. Then, you called the Cavendish Agency to find a typist -\"\n[195] She interrupted him. [196] \"I'm sorry. I didn't do that.\" [197] \"You didn't call the Cavendish Typing Agency and ask for \u2013\" Hakast's eyes widened. [198] \"I don't have a phone at home.\" [199] \"There's a phone booth at the end of the street,\" Detective Hakast pointed out. [200] \"Yes, there is. But I assure you, Detective Hakast, I don't need a typist, and I haven't \u2013 I repeat, I haven't called the Cavendish Typing Agency to make any such request.\" [201] \"Did you specifically ask for Miss Sheila Weber?\" [202] \"I've never heard of that name.\" [203] Detective Hakast stared at her, feeling quite surprised. [204] \"You didn't lock the front door when you left,\" he pointed out. [205] \"I do that often during the day.\" [206] \"Maybe someone could have walked in.\" [207] \"It seems someone did during this incident,\" Miss Pemukho said coldly. [208] \"Miss Pemukho, according to the evidence provided by the forensic expert, the man died between 1:30 and 2:45. Where were you during that time?\" [209] Miss Pemukho thought for a moment. [210] \"By 1:30, I was either out or getting ready to go out. I had to go buy some things.\" [211] \"Can you tell me exactly where you went?\" [212] \"Let me think. I went to the post office, a post office on Albion Road, to mail a package and buy some stamps; then I went to buy some everyday items, such as buttons and safety pins, at 'Field and Lane' fabric store; and then I came back. I can tell you the exact time I got home. When I got to the front gate, my cuckoo clock chimed three times 'cuckoo,' a sound that could be heard from the street.\" [213] \"What about your other clocks?\" [214] \"What did you say?\" [215] \"Your other clocks seem to be fast by more than an hour.\" [216] \"Fast? Do you mean the grandfather clock in the corner?\" [217] \"Not just that one, all the clocks in the living room are fast.\" [218] \"I don't know what you mean by 'other clocks'. There are no other clocks in this living room.\" [219] \"Chapter 3\"\n[220] Detective Hakast was flabbergasted. [221] \"Ah, so, Miss Pemshaw. What about the Dresden porcelain clock on the mantelpiece? And the exquisite French clock, gilded, and the silver travel clock, and, oh yes, the clock with 'Rosemary' written on the face.\" [222] This time, it was Miss Pemshaw's turn to be stunned. [223] \"Either you're going mad, or I am, Detective. I'm sure I don't have a Dresden porcelain clock, nor a French gilded clock, nor...what else did you say? Oh, right, the silver travel clock.\" [224] \"The silver travel clock,\" Detective Hakast mumbled. [225] \"I don't have that clock either. If you don't believe me, you can ask the woman who cleans my room. Her name is Mrs. Cotting.\" [226] Inspector Hakast was taken aback. Miss Pemarcho seemed very certain, her tone sharp and decisive, convincing. He took a moment to ponder the situation. Then he stood up. [227] \"I'd like to know, Miss Pemarcho, if you mind coming with me to the next room to see these clocks?\" [228] \"Of course not. Frankly, I'd like to see those clocks myself.\" [229] \"See?\" Hakast felt confused. [230] \"Perhaps 'perceive' would be more accurate,\" Miss Pemarcho said. \"But, Inspector, even a blind person, when they speak in ordinary terms, doesn't necessarily mean the ordinary meaning. When I say I want to 'see' those clocks, my actual intention is to 'perceive' them with my own fingers.\" [231] Detective Hakast walked out of the kitchen, with Miss Pemshaw following behind him, through the small hallway, and into the living room. The fingerprint technician looked up at him. [232] \"I've almost finished all the work, officer,\" he said. \"You can touch anything you like.\" [233] Detective Hakast nodded, picked up the small travel clock with the label \"Rosemary\" written on it, and placed it in Miss Pemshaw's hand. She carefully touched it. [234] \"This seems to be an ordinary travel clock,\" she said. \"The kind with leather covering. It's not mine, Detective Hakast. I'm quite certain it wasn't in this room when I left at half past one.\" [235] \"Thank you.\" [236] The inspector took the clock back from her hand. He carefully picked up the Dresden small porcelain clock from the mantelpiece. [237] \"Be careful,\" he said as he placed it in her hand, \"it's fragile.\" [238] Miss Pemaroo delicately touched the porcelain small clock with her delicate fingers. She shook her head. \"This must be a charming little clock,\" she said, \"but it's not mine. Where was it placed?\" [239] \"On the right side of the mantelpiece.\" [240] \"It should be one of the candlesticks there,\" Miss Pemaroo said. [241] \"Yes,\" said Hakast, \"there is one there, but it has been pushed to the edge.\" [242] \"You mean there's another clock?\" [243] \"There are two more.\" [244] Hakast took back the Dresden porcelain clock and handed her the French gilded clock. She quickly touched it and then returned it to him. [245] \"No, this one isn't mine either.\" [246] He handed her the silver clock, and she gave it back to him in the same way. [247] \"There is usually only the grandfather clock in the room, placed in the corner next to the window\u2014\"\n[248] \"Yes.\" [249] \"And there is a cuckoo clock on the wall near the door.\" [250] Hakaste found himself at a loss for words after that. He observed the woman in front of him, knowing she would not respond to his scrutiny, and felt a sense of relief. Her forehead was slightly furrowed in confusion. She suddenly spoke:\n[251] \"I don't understand what's going on. I really can't make sense of it.\" [252] She extended her hand, knowing exactly where she was in the room, and quickly found a chair to sit down. Hakaste noticed the fingerprint technician standing by the door. [253] \"Did you check all the clocks?\" he asked. [254] \"I checked everything, sir. The gilded clock has no fingerprints, which is normal since the surface of that material doesn't leave prints. The same goes for the porcelain clock. But there are no fingerprints on the leather travel clock or the silver clock, which is quite unusual. Moreover, all the clocks are not wound up, and they all stopped at the same time, 4:13 PM.\" [255] \"What about the other rooms?\" [256] \"There were three to four different types of fingerprints found in the room, and it's likely that they all belong to women. All the items found in the deceased's pockets were placed on the table.\" [257] Pointing in the direction of the person's head, he saw a small pile of items placed on the table. Hakaste walked over to take a closer look. There was a small wallet containing seven pounds, ten shillings, and some change, an unmarked silk handkerchief, a small box of digestive pills, and a business card. Hakaste bent down to look at it. [258] R. H. Kouri, Esq. [259] Great City Small Town Insurance Company\n[260] 7 Danver Street,\n[261] London W2. [262] Hakakst returned to the sofa where Miss Pemuko was sitting. [263] \"Do you ever make appointments to see someone from the insurance company?\" [264] \"Insurance company? No, absolutely not.\" [265] \"What about 'Metropolitan District Insurance Company'?\" Hakakst said. [266] Miss Pemaroo shook her head. \"I've never heard of that company.\" [267] \"Have you ever considered getting any kind of insurance?\" [268] \"No. I have fire and theft insurance with 'Jupiter Insurance Company', which has a branch here, and I don't have life insurance. I have no family or close relatives, so there's no need to insure my life.\" [269] \"I see,\" Hakaste said. \"So, does the name Kirby have any connection to you? Mr. R. H. Kirby?\" [270] \"Kirby?\" she repeated the name and shook her head. \"That's not a common name, is it? No, I don't think I've ever heard that name or known anyone by that name. Is that the name of the deceased?\" [271] \"Maybe,\" said Hakaste. [272] Miss Pemmerhoven hesitated for a moment. Then she said:\n[273] \"Should I go, touch\u2014\"\n[274] He quickly understood her meaning. [275] \"You mean to 'feel' it yourself, Miss Pemmerhoven? Is that too much to ask of you? I'm not very good at doing such things, but compared to a verbal description, your fingers might give you a better idea of what the person looked like.\" [276] \"Yes, that's what I mean,\" said Miss Pemuk. \"I admit that doing this won't be pleasant, but if you think it will be helpful, I'm willing to do it.\" [277] \"Thank you,\" said Hakast. \"I'll take you there.\" [278] He led her around the sofa, indicating for her to kneel down and then gently guiding her hand to the dead man's face. She was very calm and emotionless. Her fingers ran over the dead man's hair and eyes, lingering for a moment at the left ear before moving on to the bridge of the nose, mouth, and chin. Then she shook her head and stood up. [279] \"I have a clear image of his appearance now,\" she said. \"But I'm sure I've never seen him before, I don't recognize him.\" [280] The fingerprint examiner packed up his toolbox, left the room, and poked his head back in. [281] \"They're going to move him,\" he said, indicating the corpse. \"Can he be moved?\" [282] \"Move him,\" Detective Hakaste said. \"Come and sit over here, Miss Pemukho.\" [283] He arranged her to sit in a chair in the corner. Two men entered the room. Soon, Mr. Kuri's body was carried out. Detective Hakaste went outside to the front door and then returned to the living room. He sat next to Miss Pemukho. [284] \"This is quite a strange case, Miss Pemukho,\" he said. \"I'd like to go over the key points of the incident again to make sure I have it right. If I've said anything wrong, please correct me. You didn't have any appointments for any visitors today, you didn't consult or intend to take out any kind of insurance, and you didn't receive any letters saying that an insurance company's representative would be visiting you today, right?\" [285] \"Absolutely correct.\" [286] \"You did not need a stenographer's service, and you did not call the Calverdish Document Service and request someone to come here at three o'clock.\" [287] \"That is correct.\" [288] \"When you left the house at approximately one thirty, there were only two clocks in the house, the cuckoo clock and the grandfather clock. There were no other clocks.\" [289] Just as Miss Pemaroux was about to answer \"yes,\" she stopped. [290] \"If I am required to give a completely accurate answer, I cannot guarantee it. Since I cannot see, I cannot be certain of anything that was not in the house when I