s pockets. Miss Pemerton, think carefully now, think of anything related to clocks or any suggestions about time. Four thirteen. Fourteen minutes past four. Is that what you meant?\" [300] She shook her head. [301] \"I've been trying to tell myself it was a madman or someone who got the wrong house, but even then, it doesn't explain everything. Inspector, I can't help you.\" [302] A young policeman poked his head in. Hakaste went out. The young policeman waited in the hallway, and they walked together to the front door, talking for a few minutes. [303] \"You can take this young lady home now,\" Hakaste said. \"The address is 14 Palmerston Road.\" [304] He came back, walked into the dining room. The door to the kitchen was open, and he could hear Miss Pemaroo washing something in the sink. He stood at the door. [305] \"I want to take those clocks with me, Miss Pemaroo. I'll give you a receipt.\" [306] \"Of course, Detective, they weren't mine to begin with.\" [307] Hakast turns to Miss Webber. [308] \"You can go home now, Miss Webber. The police car will take you.\" [309] Miss Webber and Mr. Collins stand up. [310] \"Take her to the car, Mr. Collins?\" Hakast says, pulling a chair up to the table and quickly writing a receipt. [311] Colin and Shira left and walked down the path. Shira suddenly stopped. [312] \"My gloves. I forgot them\u2014\"\n[313] \"I'll get them for you.\" [314] \"No, I know where I put them. Now I'm not worried, they've moved 'it' away.\" [315] She ran back and came out shortly.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."